1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,988
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,988 --> 00:00:03,476
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,476 --> 00:00:05,460
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:14,520
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Hello.

5
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:17,240
OK, I'm just going to start.

6
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:23,320
So here's a question.

7
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,960
And I'm genuinely asking, would you

8
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:29,700
rather to scale the width or the depth of your neural network?

9
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,540
Does anyone have any thoughts?

10
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,480
Wait, someone said something.

11
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:43,600
I heard someone say depth.

12
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:44,400
AUDIENCE: Depth.

13
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:45,347
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Why?

14
00:00:45,347 --> 00:00:46,180
AUDIENCE: No reason.

15
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:47,720
I know someone said it earlier.

16
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:50,012
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Because you've heard "deep learning,"

17
00:00:50,012 --> 00:00:51,560
so it sounds-- not wider, yeah.

18
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:53,920
[LAUGHTER]

19
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:54,420
OK.

20
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:56,320
But it's not really clear.

21
00:00:56,320 --> 00:01:00,630
How do you even answer a question like that?

22
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:01,830
I don't know.

23
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:06,510
It's something we got to think about.

24
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:14,430
So in today's lecture, we're going to ask this question of,

25
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:17,670
what class of functions can a neural network express?

26
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:19,710
And you may have heard this statement

27
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:22,150
that neural networks are universal function

28
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:23,750
approximators.

29
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:28,430
So we're going to think about what that means.

30
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:32,950
And if a neural network is a universal function approximator

31
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:34,990
and it can approximate any function that we're

32
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:37,430
interested in, then how should we make decisions

33
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:39,390
about the architecture?

34
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:42,270
Does it matter how many layers there are?

35
00:01:42,270 --> 00:01:46,550
Maybe a few layers is enough if you make them really wide.

36
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:49,710
Should you have lots of layers?

37
00:01:49,710 --> 00:01:52,090
And ultimately, what do you want to do in practice?

38
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:54,310
And does thinking about neural networks

39
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:57,310
through this lens of universal function approximation

40
00:01:57,310 --> 00:02:00,340
actually help you in practice?

41
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:03,980
I'm not going to claim to answer all of these questions,

42
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:08,699
but at least try to provide some beginning of a framework, one

43
00:02:08,699 --> 00:02:10,440
framework for thinking about them.

44
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:19,140
So in machine learning, you can think of it a bit

45
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:20,200
like we have a puzzle.

46
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,080
And there are different pieces of the puzzle.

47
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:23,538
We're not going to solve the puzzle

48
00:02:23,538 --> 00:02:27,820
until we understand all of the pieces and put them together.

49
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:32,820
And I broke it up here into three pieces.

50
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:38,882
Let's see, so the first is approximation.

51
00:02:38,882 --> 00:02:40,340
This is the question of, does there

52
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:44,260
exist a neural network in my model family

53
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:45,520
that fits the training data?

54
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:47,460
So given my architecture, can it even

55
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:49,540
represent the function that I want,

56
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:51,700
that fits the training data?

57
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:54,000
The second question is optimization.

58
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,070
So let's suppose it does exist.

59
00:02:56,070 --> 00:03:00,490
Then that's the question of, can I find it?

60
00:03:00,490 --> 00:03:02,270
And third is generalization.

61
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:03,390
So suppose it exists.

62
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:04,930
Suppose I can find it.

63
00:03:04,930 --> 00:03:08,930
Then it's like, does it do well on unseen data?

64
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:11,130
So I really want all of those things

65
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:14,490
to happen if I'm going to solve my machine learning problem.

66
00:03:14,490 --> 00:03:16,570
But in this lecture, we're only going

67
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:22,068
to look at the first question about approximation.

68
00:03:22,068 --> 00:03:24,110
But just to remind you that there is this puzzle.

69
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:25,370
We really want to solve the whole puzzle

70
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:26,830
and put the pieces together.

71
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:33,930
So here is a motivating problem.

72
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:36,610
So imagine I have two-dimensional data.

73
00:03:36,610 --> 00:03:39,290
It's got two coordinates, x1 and x2.

74
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:45,010
And there's two classes, the red crosses and the green circles.

75
00:03:45,010 --> 00:03:48,210
And I've come up with my, think of this

76
00:03:48,210 --> 00:03:50,450
as a one-layer neural net.

77
00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:53,790
It's just the w-transpose x plus b.

78
00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:56,080
And then I take ReLU of that.

79
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:58,460
If that's my model, can I fit this data?

80
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:02,320
What's your name, sir?

81
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:02,982
AUDIENCE: Matt.

82
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:03,940
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Matt?

83
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:04,280
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

84
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,780
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Matt is saying no.

85
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:06,960
Why?

86
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:10,560
AUDIENCE: Don't you need two linear separation planes?

87
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,060
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

88
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:15,440
Because this thing, there's only one hyperplane

89
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:18,120
defined by w-transpose x.

90
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:21,540
And ReLU does not distort the shape of the hyperplane.

91
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:23,860
It just applies nonlinearity on the output.

92
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:28,620
This is basically a linear separator.

93
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:31,980
And this data is not linearly separable.

94
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:36,800
So this is supposed to be recap from lecture one.

95
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,860
So hopefully, it's reasonable.

96
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:42,120
But we cannot linearly separate this.

97
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:44,680
But what if I had a two-layer neural net?

98
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:47,560
Do you think I could maybe do it with two layers?

99
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:48,720
Potentially?

100
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:49,240
Yeah.

101
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:49,540
OK.

102
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:50,940
So I think we can do it with two layers,

103
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:52,398
but you may need to think about it.

104
00:04:52,398 --> 00:04:54,470
But this is just supposed to be a refresher.

105
00:04:54,470 --> 00:04:56,670
But it's saying we've got a kind of function

106
00:04:56,670 --> 00:04:59,272
that we're trying to fit, and we have a model family.

107
00:04:59,272 --> 00:05:01,230
And we're asking, does there exist the function

108
00:05:01,230 --> 00:05:04,470
in that model family that can fit that data?

109
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:06,150
So it's a kind of warm-up for thinking

110
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:12,030
about this idea of function approximation.

111
00:05:12,030 --> 00:05:14,430
I have a second motivating problem.

112
00:05:14,430 --> 00:05:19,350
Does anyone recognize this function?

113
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:21,270
You recognize it?

114
00:05:21,270 --> 00:05:22,210
What is it?

115
00:05:22,210 --> 00:05:25,190
AUDIENCE: Isn't it Weierstrass' function?

116
00:05:25,190 --> 00:05:26,390
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

117
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:28,830
It's Weierstrass' function, which

118
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:31,250
is everywhere continuous but nowhere differentiable.

119
00:05:31,250 --> 00:05:34,150
It's a classic example of a pathological function.

120
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:36,870
And the question I wanted to ask is, do you

121
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:39,530
think you could fit this function with a neural network?

122
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:47,590
Does anyone have a thought?

123
00:05:47,590 --> 00:05:48,190
Matt?

124
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:51,540
AUDIENCE: Wouldn't you have to go to infinity?

125
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:53,420
It would be like asymptotically, I guess?

126
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:54,420
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: It seems like you would need

127
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,760
some kind of asymptotic thing.

128
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:58,560
Yeah, honestly, I don't know what the answer is to this.

129
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:02,020
I just thought it was an interesting function.

130
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:05,180
And it's interesting to think, can I fit it with a neural net?

131
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:08,380
I guess I suppose the answer is, maybe.

132
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:11,140
And I thought a good final project, if someone wants

133
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:14,340
an idea, is to take all these pathological examples

134
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:15,900
that Weierstrass was thinking about

135
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:18,160
and see if you can fit them with a neural net.

136
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:21,260
But anyway, this is just more motivation, just something

137
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:23,300
to think about.

138
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:25,160
OK, so this is a fractal function.

139
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:26,880
It's defined recursively.

140
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:28,100
It's self-similar.

141
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:30,340
If you zoom into any piece of the function,

142
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:32,160
it resembles the whole function.

143
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:39,460
So we want to now formalize this approximation problem

144
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:42,540
that we've been talking about.

145
00:06:42,540 --> 00:06:47,300
So given a family of curves G--

146
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:49,490
we think of these as the curves that we

147
00:06:49,490 --> 00:06:52,410
want to be able to approximate ideally.

148
00:06:52,410 --> 00:06:54,910
And we can choose what we want G to be.

149
00:06:54,910 --> 00:06:58,670
We could choose it to rule out Weierstrass' function.

150
00:06:58,670 --> 00:07:00,590
We could ask that G is differentiable.

151
00:07:00,590 --> 00:07:04,730
Or we're free to pick whatever family of curves

152
00:07:04,730 --> 00:07:05,950
we're interested in.

153
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:08,050
That's up to us.

154
00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:10,490
And then we think of a family of neural networks

155
00:07:10,490 --> 00:07:14,030
F. This is like, I'm going to say,

156
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:17,410
I care about a five-layer MLP, a five-layer multilayer

157
00:07:17,410 --> 00:07:19,370
perceptron.

158
00:07:19,370 --> 00:07:20,670
That could be my family.

159
00:07:20,670 --> 00:07:22,650
So in other words, a neural architecture

160
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:26,050
could specify a family of functions.

161
00:07:26,050 --> 00:07:28,730
And then I want to say, if I pick any curve

162
00:07:28,730 --> 00:07:32,330
in my space of functions or set of functions

163
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:34,250
that I'm interested in, does there

164
00:07:34,250 --> 00:07:38,570
exist a neural network in my family of neural networks

165
00:07:38,570 --> 00:07:44,490
that can fit that function to some small error?

166
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:48,080
And we can come up with different notions of error.

167
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:50,800
And OK, here, epsilon, you should think of epsilon

168
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:52,280
as a small number.

169
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:55,660
So we choose epsilon, and we ask,

170
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:59,960
can we approximate any function in the family of curves G

171
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:02,880
to less than some small error?

172
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:05,760
And we have to ask, what do we mean by the error?

173
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:09,440
So we could pick whatever metric we want.

174
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:14,100
So one example, we could call the L-infinity error,

175
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:19,360
which would be the max over inputs of f of x minus g of x,

176
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:20,940
and then take the absolute value.

177
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:26,080
So this, think of it like L-infinity norm,

178
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:27,960
but for functions.

179
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:30,680
So it's the max discrepancy between the two functions

180
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:33,400
anywhere on the input space.

181
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:38,720
But another equally valid error measure, we

182
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:41,539
could call this the L infinity measure.

183
00:08:41,539 --> 00:08:44,920
Another measure would be the L1 measure,

184
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:47,190
which would be the integral over the input

185
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:51,110
space of the absolute value of f of x minus g of x.

186
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:52,590
So that would be another valid way

187
00:08:52,590 --> 00:08:54,673
to measure discrepancy, which is, I integrate over

188
00:08:54,673 --> 00:08:58,910
the function and just sum up all the differences

189
00:08:58,910 --> 00:09:00,210
along the whole axis.

190
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:06,270
So in this lecture, just for the purposes

191
00:09:06,270 --> 00:09:08,730
of having something nice to present in the lecture,

192
00:09:08,730 --> 00:09:12,310
we're going to pick a particular family of curves G,

193
00:09:12,310 --> 00:09:16,150
that we're interested in whether neural nets can

194
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:17,670
approximate this family.

195
00:09:17,670 --> 00:09:21,470
And these are the Lipschitz continuous functions.

196
00:09:21,470 --> 00:09:25,790
So has anyone-- well, I think it's

197
00:09:25,790 --> 00:09:28,390
nice to teach you about Lipschitz functions regardless

198
00:09:28,390 --> 00:09:29,810
of neural net approximation.

199
00:09:29,810 --> 00:09:32,050
So that's another nice reason to tell you about this.

200
00:09:32,050 --> 00:09:34,930
So let's just think what a Lipschitz continuous function

201
00:09:34,930 --> 00:09:35,430
is.

202
00:09:35,430 --> 00:09:39,093
It's a particular notion of continuity.

203
00:09:39,093 --> 00:09:40,510
So what we're going to do is we're

204
00:09:40,510 --> 00:09:48,260
going to say that a function g from real numbers

205
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:51,040
to real numbers is L-Lipschitz.

206
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:52,380
So L is a number.

207
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:53,920
It's like, 10 or 5.

208
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:57,220
But it just measures how Lipschitz the function is.

209
00:09:57,220 --> 00:10:04,060
We'll say it's L-Lipschitz if for any input point x,

210
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:08,740
the absolute value of g of x plus delta x minus g of x--

211
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:11,560
so think, if I change the inputs by a delta x,

212
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:13,420
I'm going to change the function.

213
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:15,900
And the amount that the function changes

214
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:18,860
is bounded by the Lipschitz constant times

215
00:10:18,860 --> 00:10:20,900
the size of delta x.

216
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:25,860
So this we call Lipschitz continuity of g.

217
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:30,100
And there's an intuitive way to think about what this is saying.

218
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:33,140
Imagine taking delta x, take the limit

219
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:35,820
that delta x becomes really small.

220
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:39,160
Can anyone recognize what this looks a little bit like,

221
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:41,560
if we take the limit that delta x becomes small?

222
00:10:45,063 --> 00:10:46,730
AUDIENCE: Is it that the slope is always

223
00:10:46,730 --> 00:10:48,327
less than some constant L?

224
00:10:48,327 --> 00:10:49,410
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

225
00:10:49,410 --> 00:10:50,550
Yes, exactly.

226
00:10:50,550 --> 00:10:53,170
So you should recognize that if I take the limit that delta

227
00:10:53,170 --> 00:10:57,130
x goes to 0, it's a bit like saying that the derivative of g

228
00:10:57,130 --> 00:11:00,450
is bounded by L. You should see a definition

229
00:11:00,450 --> 00:11:03,130
of the derivative hiding in here.

230
00:11:03,130 --> 00:11:05,130
So Lipschitz continuity is a kind

231
00:11:05,130 --> 00:11:08,770
of generalization of the notion of having a bounded derivative.

232
00:11:08,770 --> 00:11:10,270
Well, it may be equivalent actually,

233
00:11:10,270 --> 00:11:11,645
but you need to think about that.

234
00:11:11,645 --> 00:11:13,410
And just to draw a picture, let's

235
00:11:13,410 --> 00:11:17,010
suppose that we set x to 0.

236
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:21,730
So we think about this x as being 0.

237
00:11:21,730 --> 00:11:26,450
And then we ask how large can g of delta x be.

238
00:11:26,450 --> 00:11:29,290
And what this definition says, if the function is L-Lipschitz,

239
00:11:29,290 --> 00:11:34,950
we think about drawing the line like y equals Lx.

240
00:11:34,950 --> 00:11:41,440
And similarly, we draw the other line y equals minus Lx.

241
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:44,640
And this definition implies that whatever function

242
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:47,100
we have-- oops, it's not supposed to be that squiggly.

243
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:51,600
It should be reasonably not too-- anyway.

244
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:54,900
The function had better lie within those bounds.

245
00:11:54,900 --> 00:11:58,100
So the implication of the definition,

246
00:11:58,100 --> 00:11:59,900
if the function goes through the origin,

247
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:02,940
it has to lie within those bounds.

248
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:05,140
But the same argument applies at any point.

249
00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:08,660
So it places a cone kind of shape maybe,

250
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:10,080
or a kind of bow tie.

251
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:12,440
And the function has to belong to that bow tie

252
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:15,240
at any point along the function, if that makes sense.

253
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:19,000
You imagine translating the bow tie along.

254
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,700
So that's Lipschitz continuous.

255
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:23,600
And now I thought, well, another great thing to introduce you to

256
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:27,920
is how to generalize that notion to functions

257
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:29,700
with multiple inputs.

258
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:32,600
So now we're going to think about generalizing

259
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:38,480
this Lipschitz notion for g going from Rd to R.

260
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:43,030
And basically the inputs are now going to be in Rd,

261
00:12:43,030 --> 00:12:46,510
and the delta x's are going to be in Rd.

262
00:12:46,510 --> 00:12:48,970
And you see we don't really need to change anything here.

263
00:12:48,970 --> 00:12:50,530
The output is still one dimensional,

264
00:12:50,530 --> 00:12:51,990
so that still makes sense.

265
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:56,150
But this doesn't really make sense,

266
00:12:56,150 --> 00:12:58,610
because you can't take the absolute value of a vector.

267
00:12:58,610 --> 00:13:00,690
Or maybe you can, but that's not what we want.

268
00:13:03,750 --> 00:13:05,430
What we're going to do is we're going

269
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:09,630
to change the absolute value to the norm of the vector delta x.

270
00:13:09,630 --> 00:13:12,070
And I'm going to pick a particular norm that I really

271
00:13:12,070 --> 00:13:15,190
like called the RMS norm.

272
00:13:15,190 --> 00:13:16,750
So now, do you see that this is now

273
00:13:16,750 --> 00:13:18,830
a valid definition, as long as I define

274
00:13:18,830 --> 00:13:21,910
what I mean by the RMS norm?

275
00:13:21,910 --> 00:13:24,202
So the idea is that because the x is a vectors,

276
00:13:24,202 --> 00:13:25,910
we need to measure the size of the vector

277
00:13:25,910 --> 00:13:27,830
on the right-hand side.

278
00:13:27,830 --> 00:13:34,710
So I'm going to define the RMS norm of a vector x

279
00:13:34,710 --> 00:13:38,700
to be the square root of 1 over d, which

280
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:41,540
is the dimension of the vector, times the sum

281
00:13:41,540 --> 00:13:47,740
of the coordinates xi squared, which we can also

282
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:50,140
think of as just being 1 over square root d

283
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:54,540
times the Euclidean norm of x.

284
00:13:54,540 --> 00:13:57,340
So I just want to introduce you to the idea

285
00:13:57,340 --> 00:13:59,560
that if you have an object like a vector,

286
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:03,420
there's many different ways to talk about how large it is.

287
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:05,660
The Euclidean norm is one example,

288
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:09,460
but the Euclidean norm is kind of a dimensional object.

289
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:11,700
You can think about the RMS norm as a kind

290
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:13,880
of non-dimensional analog of the Euclidean norm.

291
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:16,980
Because if all the entries of the vector are 1, then

292
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:20,260
the RMS norm is 1, whereas the Euclidean norm would be like,

293
00:14:20,260 --> 00:14:21,340
square root d.

294
00:14:21,340 --> 00:14:23,340
But anyway, the point of this slide

295
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:25,420
is just to generalize Lipschitzness

296
00:14:25,420 --> 00:14:27,120
to functions with multiple inputs,

297
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:29,660
and to point out that there's many different possible ways

298
00:14:29,660 --> 00:14:32,780
to measure the size of something.

299
00:14:32,780 --> 00:14:39,130
OK, but why were we even talking about this?

300
00:14:39,130 --> 00:14:40,970
The point is that in this lecture,

301
00:14:40,970 --> 00:14:43,130
in the first part of this lecture,

302
00:14:43,130 --> 00:14:48,930
we are going to prove something akin to a universal function

303
00:14:48,930 --> 00:14:52,170
approximation theorem.

304
00:14:52,170 --> 00:14:55,330
And the class of functions g that we're

305
00:14:55,330 --> 00:14:59,690
interested in approximating is going

306
00:14:59,690 --> 00:15:02,290
to be the class of L-Lipschitz functions

307
00:15:02,290 --> 00:15:04,390
that map from the hypercube.

308
00:15:04,390 --> 00:15:08,210
The domain or the input space is the hypercube

309
00:15:08,210 --> 00:15:09,850
to the real numbers.

310
00:15:09,850 --> 00:15:11,310
So this is the hypercube.

311
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:12,450
And these are the reals.

312
00:15:12,450 --> 00:15:15,330
And we're only considering L-Lipschitz functions, so

313
00:15:15,330 --> 00:15:18,410
functions with bounded derivatives.

314
00:15:18,410 --> 00:15:21,570
And what we're going to say is that if we pick an error

315
00:15:21,570 --> 00:15:24,770
tolerance that we're interested in, then there exists

316
00:15:24,770 --> 00:15:30,410
a three-layer ReLU network with a certain number of units such

317
00:15:30,410 --> 00:15:34,960
that this integral notion of error--

318
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:37,172
summing up all the little pieces and adding up

319
00:15:37,172 --> 00:15:38,880
all the absolute values of the difference

320
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:40,880
between our neural net and the function

321
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:46,720
g-- that integral is less than apparently 2 epsilon.

322
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:49,900
Does anyone have any questions about this?

323
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:54,840
We could dwell on it for a moment.

324
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:58,380
This is our goal basically for the next 20 minutes.

325
00:15:58,380 --> 00:16:01,320
Hopefully, we're just going to try to prove this statement.

326
00:16:01,320 --> 00:16:04,020
Why should we be interested in such a statement?

327
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:05,340
That's another question.

328
00:16:09,440 --> 00:16:10,460
Yeah, go ahead.

329
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:15,360
AUDIENCE: The 0, 1, the mapping from 0 to 1 definition,

330
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:17,580
is that taking only values from 0 and 1?

331
00:16:17,580 --> 00:16:20,120
Is that what that means?

332
00:16:20,120 --> 00:16:22,760
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: The interval 0, 1, with square brackets

333
00:16:22,760 --> 00:16:27,040
refers to the interval of the real line just between 0 and 1.

334
00:16:27,040 --> 00:16:29,980
So that's just picking out an interval on the real line.

335
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:32,000
And then raising it to the power d

336
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,930
means that in all the dimensions of our d-dimensional space,

337
00:16:34,930 --> 00:16:36,210
we pick out that interval.

338
00:16:36,210 --> 00:16:38,070
So it's like picking out a cube in three

339
00:16:38,070 --> 00:16:40,070
dimensions or a hypercube.

340
00:16:40,070 --> 00:16:44,590
It's just supposed to be an abstract way of writing down

341
00:16:44,590 --> 00:16:48,110
the hypercube in d-dimensional space, where all the axes are

342
00:16:48,110 --> 00:16:51,550
between 0 and 1.

343
00:16:51,550 --> 00:16:54,277
AUDIENCE: What is N here?

344
00:16:54,277 --> 00:16:55,110
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: N?

345
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:03,240
Capital N is my symbol for the number of units.

346
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:07,589
By units, we mean the number of neurons in the ReLU MLP.

347
00:17:07,589 --> 00:17:11,750
So N is the number of neurons in the three-layer network.

348
00:17:11,750 --> 00:17:12,944
AUDIENCE: OK.

349
00:17:12,944 --> 00:17:14,569
AUDIENCE: I have a couple of questions.

350
00:17:14,569 --> 00:17:18,150
So first, are those neurons per layer

351
00:17:18,150 --> 00:17:20,435
or are they the total number in the whole network?

352
00:17:20,435 --> 00:17:21,810
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: It's the total.

353
00:17:21,810 --> 00:17:24,015
But honestly, because it's three layers,

354
00:17:24,015 --> 00:17:25,890
for the purpose of understanding the lecture,

355
00:17:25,890 --> 00:17:28,490
you can just pretend that the number 3 is the number 1.

356
00:17:28,490 --> 00:17:31,560
And that kind of question doesn't really matter so much.

357
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:38,220
But I really mean actually the sum total of the neurons.

358
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:41,620
AUDIENCE: So a second question is,

359
00:17:41,620 --> 00:17:44,000
when you say three-layer ReLU network,

360
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,560
what precisely do you mean by that?

361
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:48,780
So are there three value functions in it?

362
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:49,620
For example--

363
00:17:49,620 --> 00:17:51,453
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: I precisely mean the thing

364
00:17:51,453 --> 00:17:54,540
with three weight matrices and two value

365
00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:56,500
functions that follow the first weight matrix

366
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:57,760
and the second weight matrix.

367
00:17:57,760 --> 00:18:00,040
And then the third weight matrix doesn't have a ReLU.

368
00:18:00,040 --> 00:18:00,582
AUDIENCE: OK.

369
00:18:00,582 --> 00:18:03,100
And then the final question I had is,

370
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:05,488
this theorem seems very specific.

371
00:18:05,488 --> 00:18:07,780
I was just wondering if you could speak a little bit as

372
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,383
to what you brought up-- why do we care about this?

373
00:18:10,383 --> 00:18:11,300
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

374
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:12,020
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

375
00:18:12,020 --> 00:18:14,040
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So that's a great question.

376
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:17,940
So the reason is because the way to prove this theorem

377
00:18:17,940 --> 00:18:19,500
is not super involved.

378
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:23,100
So the point is to show you an example of such a theorem.

379
00:18:23,100 --> 00:18:26,460
And you can see the whole proof and how it works.

380
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:29,060
And then I'll point you to some other references

381
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:30,890
where they do different calculations

382
00:18:30,890 --> 00:18:32,030
or different things.

383
00:18:32,030 --> 00:18:33,670
It's just to show you an example.

384
00:18:33,670 --> 00:18:36,470
And then we're really going to think about, is this relevant?

385
00:18:36,470 --> 00:18:38,710
I'm not claiming that this is an important result.

386
00:18:38,710 --> 00:18:40,755
I'm just saying it's something you can prove.

387
00:18:40,755 --> 00:18:42,130
And then we'll think a bit about,

388
00:18:42,130 --> 00:18:44,190
is this an important result or not?

389
00:18:47,170 --> 00:18:47,855
Yeah?

390
00:18:47,855 --> 00:18:49,730
AUDIENCE: Does this theorem apply to networks

391
00:18:49,730 --> 00:18:51,610
that are more than one layer?

392
00:18:51,610 --> 00:18:54,930
In our notes from the first lecture,

393
00:18:54,930 --> 00:18:57,690
there was a section about universal approximation theorem

394
00:18:57,690 --> 00:18:59,770
for a single value layer, where you have

395
00:18:59,770 --> 00:19:02,950
a given amount of value units.

396
00:19:02,950 --> 00:19:05,850
And in that single layer, you could approximate a function

397
00:19:05,850 --> 00:19:07,270
to arbitrary precision.

398
00:19:07,270 --> 00:19:08,450
So does that apply?

399
00:19:08,450 --> 00:19:11,530
Does this theorem apply to layers that are more than one?

400
00:19:11,530 --> 00:19:14,510
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: This applies to a three-layer ReLU network.

401
00:19:14,510 --> 00:19:17,430
So this is not the most general thing that you can prove.

402
00:19:17,430 --> 00:19:19,950
The result that you're talking about is a different result.

403
00:19:19,950 --> 00:19:22,090
This is just another result where

404
00:19:22,090 --> 00:19:24,690
there's a proof, which we can put into some

405
00:19:24,690 --> 00:19:25,950
slides and show to you.

406
00:19:25,950 --> 00:19:28,240
It's just to get you thinking about how

407
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:29,540
such a result could look.

408
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:30,820
But there are other results.

409
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:32,653
There are probably more interesting versions

410
00:19:32,653 --> 00:19:34,880
of this result. This is just a result,

411
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:38,800
but it's about three-layer network.

412
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:45,200
AUDIENCE: So for this, is it possible to shift the interval

413
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:46,962
or scale the interval?

414
00:19:46,962 --> 00:19:47,920
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

415
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:49,960
Anytime you see the hypercube, you should think,

416
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:51,980
I can probably just rescale that.

417
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:54,380
And I'm going to change some scaling numbers.

418
00:19:54,380 --> 00:19:57,302
I'd probably change maybe what the Lipschitz constant is.

419
00:19:57,302 --> 00:19:58,760
Yeah, you should be able to rescale

420
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:02,680
it to be any hypercube that has arbitrary

421
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:05,020
sizes in different dimensions, basically,

422
00:20:05,020 --> 00:20:08,800
or hypercuboid or something like that.

423
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:09,500
Yes.

424
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:17,500
AUDIENCE: Why do we want to constrain everything to cube?

425
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:20,088
Does this mean that everything should be finite?

426
00:20:20,088 --> 00:20:22,380
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, that's an interesting question.

427
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:29,310
Is that a toy thing about this theorem?

428
00:20:29,310 --> 00:20:31,910
But actually, if you think about in real deep learning,

429
00:20:31,910 --> 00:20:37,630
you usually normalize your inputs to be coordinate-wise,

430
00:20:37,630 --> 00:20:39,162
about 1 in magnitude.

431
00:20:39,162 --> 00:20:40,870
You could actually train a neural network

432
00:20:40,870 --> 00:20:42,453
on an interesting problem and actually

433
00:20:42,453 --> 00:20:45,630
project all the inputs to live within a hypercube, or maybe--

434
00:20:45,630 --> 00:20:47,170
a hypercube.

435
00:20:47,170 --> 00:20:47,850
Yeah, yeah.

436
00:20:47,850 --> 00:20:51,430
So that part is actually a realistic assumption

437
00:20:51,430 --> 00:20:54,530
that my data lives in a hypercube, for many problems,

438
00:20:54,530 --> 00:20:57,270
not for all problems.

439
00:20:57,270 --> 00:20:58,903
Yeah, last question.

440
00:20:58,903 --> 00:21:01,570
AUDIENCE: So the 1 over epsilon to the d seems pretty important.

441
00:21:01,570 --> 00:21:04,492
Do you know if you relax the L-Lipschitz that

442
00:21:04,492 --> 00:21:06,450
will take a different definition of continuous,

443
00:21:06,450 --> 00:21:09,590
do you still remain with that as the best you can do?

444
00:21:09,590 --> 00:21:13,470
Or can you do better than that power law?

445
00:21:13,470 --> 00:21:14,870
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, I imagine

446
00:21:14,870 --> 00:21:18,030
with more knowledge about the problem structure,

447
00:21:18,030 --> 00:21:20,570
you can do much better.

448
00:21:23,510 --> 00:21:26,110
I'm not trying to claim that this is an interesting result.

449
00:21:26,110 --> 00:21:27,870
And we'll talk about the limitations.

450
00:21:27,870 --> 00:21:31,210
And I think you would hope to do--

451
00:21:31,210 --> 00:21:31,925
yeah, just look.

452
00:21:31,925 --> 00:21:34,050
It's saying that you need N-- the number of neurons

453
00:21:34,050 --> 00:21:37,130
you need, you take the Lipschitz constant,

454
00:21:37,130 --> 00:21:39,930
and you raise it to the power of the dimension, which is really,

455
00:21:39,930 --> 00:21:41,570
that could potentially be massive.

456
00:21:41,570 --> 00:21:44,830
And also the smaller the error of tolerance you want,

457
00:21:44,830 --> 00:21:46,330
the bigger the number of neurons you

458
00:21:46,330 --> 00:21:49,550
need in a way that depends exponentially on the dimension.

459
00:21:49,550 --> 00:21:55,530
So that's really bad dimension dependence.

460
00:21:55,530 --> 00:22:00,963
And we don't, in practice, really want to do that ever.

461
00:22:00,963 --> 00:22:03,130
Yeah, it's great to point out that the result itself

462
00:22:03,130 --> 00:22:06,050
is not even the best possible result we might hope for.

463
00:22:06,050 --> 00:22:07,450
Whether we can do better probably

464
00:22:07,450 --> 00:22:09,210
depends on structure in the problem.

465
00:22:09,210 --> 00:22:11,210
And I don't know even if under these conditions,

466
00:22:11,210 --> 00:22:15,610
maybe there's something better, but I'm not sure.

467
00:22:15,610 --> 00:22:19,230
OK, let's just plow ahead now for a little bit.

468
00:22:19,230 --> 00:22:24,440
So the strategy that we're going to adopt for proving this result

469
00:22:24,440 --> 00:22:27,080
is, first of all, we're going to pretend that our inputs are

470
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:27,860
one dimensional.

471
00:22:27,860 --> 00:22:30,402
We're just going to treat the case of one-dimensional inputs,

472
00:22:30,402 --> 00:22:31,520
one-dimensional outputs.

473
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:34,380
And we're going to forget about ReLU networks for a little bit.

474
00:22:34,380 --> 00:22:37,880
And we're going to think about approximating our function just

475
00:22:37,880 --> 00:22:41,620
with rectangles that we build up.

476
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:43,440
And you can see that you can approximate

477
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:47,560
a function by taking these rectangles

478
00:22:47,560 --> 00:22:50,200
and putting them like this.

479
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:53,157
And you can see that as you make the width of each rectangle

480
00:22:53,157 --> 00:22:55,240
smaller, you get a better and better approximation

481
00:22:55,240 --> 00:22:57,440
to your function.

482
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:59,960
And that's something we can quantify quite easily.

483
00:22:59,960 --> 00:23:01,600
The second step is then we're going

484
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:03,600
to generalize that rectangle construction

485
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:07,760
to multidimensional inputs, still ignoring ReLU networks.

486
00:23:07,760 --> 00:23:10,440
And then the third step is we're going

487
00:23:10,440 --> 00:23:13,800
to show that with a two-layer ReLU network,

488
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:17,580
you can approximate a hyperrectangle of that kind.

489
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:19,880
So then you can just use the third layer of the network

490
00:23:19,880 --> 00:23:22,670
to linearly combine all of your rectangles

491
00:23:22,670 --> 00:23:24,950
and you'll be able to approximate your function.

492
00:23:24,950 --> 00:23:26,852
So this is the strategy.

493
00:23:26,852 --> 00:23:28,810
First approximate the function with rectangles,

494
00:23:28,810 --> 00:23:31,390
then approximate it with hyperrectangles.

495
00:23:31,390 --> 00:23:34,390
Then show that a two-layer ReLU network can

496
00:23:34,390 --> 00:23:36,170
approximate a hyperrectangle.

497
00:23:38,950 --> 00:23:42,570
OK, let's just plow on, in the interest of time.

498
00:23:45,470 --> 00:23:50,150
So the first step is to construct

499
00:23:50,150 --> 00:23:52,390
these rectangular strips.

500
00:23:52,390 --> 00:23:55,710
Each strip is centered on a discrete grid point.

501
00:23:55,710 --> 00:23:58,910
And you can think that this is like building a function-- f

502
00:23:58,910 --> 00:24:03,210
of x is the sum over alpha i times,

503
00:24:03,210 --> 00:24:05,990
let's call this the i-th indicator.

504
00:24:05,990 --> 00:24:10,670
What you should think is that alpha i is measuring the height

505
00:24:10,670 --> 00:24:14,030
of the i-th rectangle alpha i.

506
00:24:14,030 --> 00:24:17,910
And indicator i is the function, which

507
00:24:17,910 --> 00:24:22,860
is 1, if the input is in this interval, and 0 otherwise.

508
00:24:22,860 --> 00:24:28,180
And then if I sum up a bunch of these indicators of this form,

509
00:24:28,180 --> 00:24:31,160
that corresponds to this approximation to the function.

510
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:35,320
Is that clear?

511
00:24:35,320 --> 00:24:35,820
OK.

512
00:24:35,820 --> 00:24:39,580
So the next step is basically what we're going to ask

513
00:24:39,580 --> 00:24:41,520
is, if we approximate a function in this way--

514
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:44,540
let's say we make the left edge of the rectangle

515
00:24:44,540 --> 00:24:46,580
actually touch the function.

516
00:24:46,580 --> 00:24:52,860
We need to ask, how big is this gap?

517
00:24:52,860 --> 00:24:54,380
How big can it be?

518
00:24:54,380 --> 00:24:57,100
And the observation is--

519
00:24:57,100 --> 00:24:59,380
OK, does anyone have an idea of how

520
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:04,100
to place a constraint on how large that difference can be?

521
00:25:04,100 --> 00:25:04,600
Yeah?

522
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:06,308
AUDIENCE: We use the Lipschitz condition.

523
00:25:06,308 --> 00:25:08,297
It would just be the area of the triangle.

524
00:25:08,297 --> 00:25:09,380
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

525
00:25:09,380 --> 00:25:11,373
So the observation is that we're assuming

526
00:25:11,373 --> 00:25:13,540
that the function g that we're trying to approximate

527
00:25:13,540 --> 00:25:14,900
is L-Lipschitz.

528
00:25:14,900 --> 00:25:17,140
Like we said, that places a bound on how large

529
00:25:17,140 --> 00:25:18,510
its derivative can be.

530
00:25:18,510 --> 00:25:21,690
And that gives us a constraint on how big this triangle can be.

531
00:25:21,690 --> 00:25:24,650
Or the triangle could go under, but it tells us

532
00:25:24,650 --> 00:25:26,630
how big this thing can be.

533
00:25:30,210 --> 00:25:33,730
And so we're going to ask, given N strips, where N,

534
00:25:33,730 --> 00:25:37,190
we can ramp it up or we can make N as big as we want.

535
00:25:37,190 --> 00:25:40,050
But given that we have N of them,

536
00:25:40,050 --> 00:25:42,130
what is the approximation error?

537
00:25:42,130 --> 00:25:45,530
And the claim is that the approximation error

538
00:25:45,530 --> 00:25:47,650
is bounded by the Lipschitz constant divided

539
00:25:47,650 --> 00:25:51,890
by 2 times the number of strips.

540
00:25:51,890 --> 00:25:55,470
And so importantly, if the Lipschitz constant gets bigger,

541
00:25:55,470 --> 00:25:58,150
then if I keep the number of strips the same,

542
00:25:58,150 --> 00:25:59,710
the error would get bigger.

543
00:25:59,710 --> 00:26:02,830
But if I make the number of rectangles larger,

544
00:26:02,830 --> 00:26:04,070
the error would go down.

545
00:26:10,170 --> 00:26:13,370
So to prove this, can you see that basically, we just need

546
00:26:13,370 --> 00:26:16,360
to do the geometry of what this triangle can look like and then

547
00:26:16,360 --> 00:26:18,320
sum it up over all the strips?

548
00:26:18,320 --> 00:26:20,960
So let's just do that quickly.

549
00:26:20,960 --> 00:26:27,720
So if we have N strips, each rectangle is width 1 over N.

550
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:31,080
And the height of the triangle, if you just

551
00:26:31,080 --> 00:26:33,340
think through the definition of Lipschitzness,

552
00:26:33,340 --> 00:26:36,400
you just multiply the width of the strip by L.

553
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:39,600
And that will give you the maximum possible height.

554
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:44,080
And then, of course, then the triangle has area--

555
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:47,040
What's the area of this triangle?

556
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:48,260
Can someone tell me?

557
00:26:48,260 --> 00:26:49,440
[LAUGHTER]

558
00:26:49,440 --> 00:26:51,180
Oh, wait I remember.

559
00:26:51,180 --> 00:26:54,140
It's that 1/2 L over N squared, right?

560
00:26:54,140 --> 00:26:56,200
The 1/2 base times height.

561
00:26:56,200 --> 00:26:58,700
And this means that the total error--

562
00:26:58,700 --> 00:27:02,760
remember that we're dealing with the L1 error of the function

563
00:27:02,760 --> 00:27:04,080
approximation--

564
00:27:04,080 --> 00:27:09,920
is just going to be like N times the error of each rectangle.

565
00:27:09,920 --> 00:27:14,100
And that corresponds to actually the area of the triangle,

566
00:27:14,100 --> 00:27:15,970
the maximum possible area of the triangle,

567
00:27:15,970 --> 00:27:17,550
which is 1/2 L over N squared.

568
00:27:17,550 --> 00:27:23,510
So this is just 1/2 L over N. OK,

569
00:27:23,510 --> 00:27:25,330
now we just need to flip things around.

570
00:27:25,330 --> 00:27:28,050
That's the largest possible thing the error could be.

571
00:27:28,050 --> 00:27:30,010
But what we wanted is a statement of the form--

572
00:27:30,010 --> 00:27:35,150
if we have this many rectangles, then the error cannot exceed

573
00:27:35,150 --> 00:27:36,190
epsilon.

574
00:27:36,190 --> 00:27:39,110
So we need to flip the statement around.

575
00:27:39,110 --> 00:27:43,710
So that corresponds, the way you do it mentally is just you say,

576
00:27:43,710 --> 00:27:47,550
epsilon is 1/2 L over N.

577
00:27:47,550 --> 00:27:49,630
So then to achieve error epsilon, we just

578
00:27:49,630 --> 00:27:50,850
rearrange this equation.

579
00:27:50,850 --> 00:27:56,190
We get N needs to be greater than or equal to 1/2 L over

580
00:27:56,190 --> 00:27:56,890
epsilon.

581
00:28:00,350 --> 00:28:04,150
And if I don't care about this factor of 1/2,

582
00:28:04,150 --> 00:28:06,035
I'm free to just also erase that factor,

583
00:28:06,035 --> 00:28:07,410
because it doesn't really matter.

584
00:28:11,270 --> 00:28:14,820
The form of this statement, is if I set N greater than L over

585
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:18,980
epsilon, then I will have an error epsilon less than 1/2 L

586
00:28:18,980 --> 00:28:22,300
over N. So I'm just rearranging the equation and being careful

587
00:28:22,300 --> 00:28:25,100
about the inequalities.

588
00:28:25,100 --> 00:28:28,440
So this is step one.

589
00:28:28,440 --> 00:28:30,020
We know how many rectangles we need

590
00:28:30,020 --> 00:28:32,780
to get a certain error in the one-dimensional case

591
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:34,460
with one-dimensional inputs.

592
00:28:34,460 --> 00:28:36,820
Let's think about how things change if we

593
00:28:36,820 --> 00:28:41,740
have multidimensional inputs.

594
00:28:41,740 --> 00:28:49,420
And the thing that changes is we now have this hyperrectangle,

595
00:28:49,420 --> 00:28:50,898
just think 3D.

596
00:28:50,898 --> 00:28:52,940
Whenever you need to think about high dimensions,

597
00:28:52,940 --> 00:28:56,520
you just think about things in three dimensions.

598
00:29:00,940 --> 00:29:04,900
What I'm doing, I'm just repeating the calculation,

599
00:29:04,900 --> 00:29:09,300
the total error, and then I'm breaking it up into pieces.

600
00:29:09,300 --> 00:29:13,470
So this piece is just the number of hyperrectangles.

601
00:29:16,770 --> 00:29:21,970
This piece is the error per hyperrectangle.

602
00:29:21,970 --> 00:29:24,970
No, we think about the hyperrectangle as having

603
00:29:24,970 --> 00:29:28,210
a little cap on top, where the hyperrectangle differs

604
00:29:28,210 --> 00:29:33,170
from the surface G.

605
00:29:33,170 --> 00:29:37,150
And we know the surface area of the top of the hyperrectangle,

606
00:29:37,150 --> 00:29:40,130
because it's just the width of the hyperrectangle raised

607
00:29:40,130 --> 00:29:42,510
to the number of input dimensions.

608
00:29:42,510 --> 00:29:44,910
So we know the surface area of the top of the rectangle.

609
00:29:44,910 --> 00:29:45,713
That's easy.

610
00:29:45,713 --> 00:29:47,130
And then we just use the Lipschitz

611
00:29:47,130 --> 00:29:49,330
constant to measure the maximum possible height

612
00:29:49,330 --> 00:29:50,615
of the hyperrectangle.

613
00:29:50,615 --> 00:29:52,490
So it's just repeating exactly the same thing

614
00:29:52,490 --> 00:29:55,910
that we did, except instead of the error being a triangle,

615
00:29:55,910 --> 00:29:58,230
it's some kind of generalization of a triangle.

616
00:29:58,230 --> 00:30:01,090
So I'll probably just leave people to think that through

617
00:30:01,090 --> 00:30:03,790
by looking at the slides, if you want to think more about that.

618
00:30:03,790 --> 00:30:06,130
But this is the height.

619
00:30:06,130 --> 00:30:07,830
Let's call it the error cap.

620
00:30:20,728 --> 00:30:22,160
Is this right?

621
00:30:24,390 --> 00:30:25,140
Yeah, it is right.

622
00:30:25,140 --> 00:30:27,098
And then if you think about, what's the surface

623
00:30:27,098 --> 00:30:29,020
area of the top of the cap?

624
00:30:29,020 --> 00:30:31,840
Well, if there's N hyperrectangles,

625
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:34,740
if I sum up all the surface areas of all of them,

626
00:30:34,740 --> 00:30:38,080
I should get 1.

627
00:30:38,080 --> 00:30:40,600
Yeah, so then the surface area of each one

628
00:30:40,600 --> 00:30:43,860
should better be 1 over N. So we'll just call this the area.

629
00:30:43,860 --> 00:30:47,000
If anyone wants to think through this more carefully, please

630
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,220
do so.

631
00:30:49,220 --> 00:30:52,150
And the claim is that we've just done the same calculation.

632
00:30:52,150 --> 00:30:54,400
The only remaining thing is to rearrange it in the way

633
00:30:54,400 --> 00:30:55,640
that we just did.

634
00:30:55,640 --> 00:31:01,120
And this would say that we should need L over epsilon

635
00:31:01,120 --> 00:31:02,480
to the d.

636
00:31:02,480 --> 00:31:05,800
So I do the same trick of setting

637
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:10,310
epsilon equal to this thing, and then just rearranging.

638
00:31:10,310 --> 00:31:15,990
Now, let's just zoom out and just try

639
00:31:15,990 --> 00:31:18,470
to assess where we're up to.

640
00:31:18,470 --> 00:31:20,270
So what we're imagining is that we now

641
00:31:20,270 --> 00:31:23,730
have a function g with multiple inputs and one output,

642
00:31:23,730 --> 00:31:27,230
which we can think of as a surface.

643
00:31:27,230 --> 00:31:29,510
That would be the case where there's

644
00:31:29,510 --> 00:31:31,210
two inputs and one output.

645
00:31:31,210 --> 00:31:32,090
It's like a surface.

646
00:31:32,090 --> 00:31:36,110
And then in higher dimensions, it's a generalization of that.

647
00:31:36,110 --> 00:31:40,070
And the claim is that if we want to approximate this

648
00:31:40,070 --> 00:31:42,750
with N hyperrectangles and we want

649
00:31:42,750 --> 00:31:45,770
to get a certain notion of the integral of the error,

650
00:31:45,770 --> 00:31:48,950
that the number that we need to get an error epsilon

651
00:31:48,950 --> 00:31:51,110
is L over epsilon to the d.

652
00:31:51,110 --> 00:31:53,750
And this should remind you a lot of the thing which appeared

653
00:31:53,750 --> 00:31:55,670
in the theorem statement.

654
00:31:55,670 --> 00:31:58,030
And the reason for that is that the next step

655
00:31:58,030 --> 00:31:59,510
is to show that we can approximate

656
00:31:59,510 --> 00:32:04,470
the rectangle with a ReLU, with a two-layer ReLU network.

657
00:32:04,470 --> 00:32:06,780
So the number of small two-layer ReLU

658
00:32:06,780 --> 00:32:09,575
networks we're going to need is L over epsilon to the d.

659
00:32:09,575 --> 00:32:11,700
And then we just need to count how many neurons are

660
00:32:11,700 --> 00:32:13,760
in each of those ReLU networks.

661
00:32:16,740 --> 00:32:24,660
So I just decided to just zoom over this.

662
00:32:24,660 --> 00:32:26,260
Because I want to show you actually

663
00:32:26,260 --> 00:32:27,780
how to construct this thing.

664
00:32:27,780 --> 00:32:32,580
But basically the claim is that there's a one-layer--

665
00:32:32,580 --> 00:32:33,220
is this one?

666
00:32:33,220 --> 00:32:35,140
OK, we would call this a two-layer ReLU

667
00:32:35,140 --> 00:32:41,540
network with a parameter c, a kind of weight c.

668
00:32:41,540 --> 00:32:43,940
And if we take c to infinity--

669
00:32:43,940 --> 00:32:46,100
which seems like a bit of a trick, but OK, that's

670
00:32:46,100 --> 00:32:47,740
what we're going to do--

671
00:32:47,740 --> 00:32:50,140
the claim is that that network converges

672
00:32:50,140 --> 00:32:55,340
to this function, which is exactly this single rectangle

673
00:32:55,340 --> 00:32:56,580
in one dimension.

674
00:32:56,580 --> 00:32:59,700
So I just want to take a bit of a risk

675
00:32:59,700 --> 00:33:05,930
by switching off and hoping that I'm able to reshare my iPad.

676
00:33:05,930 --> 00:33:12,730
I'll just try to build this thing for you.

677
00:33:12,730 --> 00:33:15,730
So this is a ReLU.

678
00:33:15,730 --> 00:33:17,450
By the way, this kind of manipulation

679
00:33:17,450 --> 00:33:20,850
is quite helpful on some of the homework problems, probably.

680
00:33:20,850 --> 00:33:21,950
This, I really recommend.

681
00:33:21,950 --> 00:33:25,030
I'm not being paid by this graphing software,

682
00:33:25,030 --> 00:33:27,690
but it's really great.

683
00:33:27,690 --> 00:33:32,130
And what I want to show you is, so here I've got ReLU of x.

684
00:33:32,130 --> 00:33:35,270
And then I'm just going to subtract ReLU of x minus 1.

685
00:33:35,270 --> 00:33:39,130
And you see it flattens it out.

686
00:33:39,130 --> 00:33:42,470
And then say I want to curve it down,

687
00:33:42,470 --> 00:33:44,490
so I subtract another ReLU.

688
00:33:44,490 --> 00:33:48,410
And then I want to flatten off that little flat bit that's

689
00:33:48,410 --> 00:33:49,030
not flat.

690
00:33:49,030 --> 00:33:50,590
So I'm just going to add a ReLU.

691
00:33:50,590 --> 00:33:53,710
And each time, I'm translating them one along.

692
00:33:56,250 --> 00:33:58,710
But now I'm like, hey, that doesn't look like a rectangle.

693
00:33:58,710 --> 00:34:00,890
The slopes are too non-slope--

694
00:34:00,890 --> 00:34:02,990
I want them to be slopier or something.

695
00:34:06,320 --> 00:34:10,300
OK, I'm going to insert a constant c, which is initially 1

696
00:34:10,300 --> 00:34:12,199
so it's not changing anything.

697
00:34:12,199 --> 00:34:14,600
And I'm just going to use this constant to increase

698
00:34:14,600 --> 00:34:18,760
the slope of all the curves.

699
00:34:18,760 --> 00:34:21,315
And let's put it between 1 and 10.

700
00:34:21,315 --> 00:34:22,940
And then I'm just going to increase it.

701
00:34:22,940 --> 00:34:26,560
And I'm like, oh, it's making the slopes slopier,

702
00:34:26,560 --> 00:34:31,719
but it's also squeezing them together.

703
00:34:31,719 --> 00:34:38,840
And so I can solve that problem just by translating them back,

704
00:34:38,840 --> 00:34:39,920
I hope.

705
00:34:39,920 --> 00:34:41,699
That doesn't look like what I wanted.

706
00:34:45,679 --> 00:34:47,242
Let's see.

707
00:34:47,242 --> 00:34:48,659
Oh, now it looks like what I want.

708
00:34:48,659 --> 00:34:51,600
So I just translated them back.

709
00:34:51,600 --> 00:34:54,659
And then I'm just going to let c get a little bigger,

710
00:34:54,659 --> 00:34:57,220
maybe 1,000, and make it a bit bigger.

711
00:34:57,220 --> 00:34:58,680
And you see, OK, I did it.

712
00:34:58,680 --> 00:35:01,000
That's just combining ReLUs.

713
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:03,270
That's technically a two-layer neural net.

714
00:35:03,270 --> 00:35:06,230
And I could approximate a square function.

715
00:35:06,230 --> 00:35:10,990
So that's what this slide is doing, which I now

716
00:35:10,990 --> 00:35:12,330
hope I can bring back.

717
00:35:16,390 --> 00:35:19,947
So that is the demo of approximating.

718
00:35:19,947 --> 00:35:22,030
And you can see that using that graphing software,

719
00:35:22,030 --> 00:35:23,470
you can just play around with things

720
00:35:23,470 --> 00:35:24,595
that you can figure it out.

721
00:35:24,595 --> 00:35:25,130
Go ahead.

722
00:35:25,130 --> 00:35:26,505
AUDIENCE: What's the relationship

723
00:35:26,505 --> 00:35:30,167
between a two-layer ReLU network and a Riemann sum?

724
00:35:30,167 --> 00:35:31,750
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, so we're going

725
00:35:31,750 --> 00:35:35,790
to use one copy of this two-layer network

726
00:35:35,790 --> 00:35:38,750
to approximate each rectangle, to approximate one

727
00:35:38,750 --> 00:35:42,230
of the rectangles in that Riemann sum kind of thing.

728
00:35:42,230 --> 00:35:43,990
And then we're going to weight each.

729
00:35:43,990 --> 00:35:47,910
And you have to insert factors to translate it to the position

730
00:35:47,910 --> 00:35:48,530
that you want.

731
00:35:48,530 --> 00:35:50,590
So you translate it, and then you multiply it

732
00:35:50,590 --> 00:35:54,290
by a little scalar to get it to be the size that you want.

733
00:35:54,290 --> 00:35:58,230
And that scalar would correspond to the third layer

734
00:35:58,230 --> 00:36:01,860
in the network, to set the size of the rectangle, how tall it is

735
00:36:01,860 --> 00:36:03,540
or what its height is.

736
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:06,026
Yeah?

737
00:36:06,026 --> 00:36:07,780
AUDIENCE: Why do we restrict ourselves

738
00:36:07,780 --> 00:36:09,560
to L-Lipschitz functions?

739
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:13,887
Wouldn't that be for any kind of function?

740
00:36:13,887 --> 00:36:16,220
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, it's to get a sense of the error

741
00:36:16,220 --> 00:36:17,520
when things are finite.

742
00:36:17,520 --> 00:36:18,620
Because we're not interested in actually

743
00:36:18,620 --> 00:36:21,120
taking the limit that the number of strips goes to infinity.

744
00:36:21,120 --> 00:36:23,280
We want to know for a finite number of strips.

745
00:36:23,280 --> 00:36:26,160
So at least that's how we're using the Lipschitzness.

746
00:36:26,160 --> 00:36:29,880
But, yeah, I think it's necessary.

747
00:36:29,880 --> 00:36:31,160
I'm not 100% sure.

748
00:36:31,160 --> 00:36:33,740
But we can maybe talk about that after the lecture.

749
00:36:33,740 --> 00:36:38,540
Let me just keep going, because I'm a bit worried about time.

750
00:36:38,540 --> 00:36:39,840
This is just half the lecture.

751
00:36:39,840 --> 00:36:42,048
And I guess we're nearly halfway through the lecture,

752
00:36:42,048 --> 00:36:43,300
so maybe we're doing OK.

753
00:36:43,300 --> 00:36:45,800
So that was a one-dimensional rectangle.

754
00:36:45,800 --> 00:36:49,740
But remember that we need to be able to approximate a rectangle

755
00:36:49,740 --> 00:36:51,900
with a hyperrectangle.

756
00:36:51,900 --> 00:36:54,200
In two dimensions, it's a kind of cube.

757
00:36:54,200 --> 00:36:56,680
And in three dimensions, it's a hypercube kind of thing.

758
00:36:56,680 --> 00:36:57,180
OK.

759
00:36:57,180 --> 00:37:00,458
But there's another trick.

760
00:37:00,458 --> 00:37:02,750
There seems like there's a lot of tricks going on here.

761
00:37:02,750 --> 00:37:04,375
But another trick is, we basically take

762
00:37:04,375 --> 00:37:07,992
a hyperrectangle that's aligned with one axis,

763
00:37:07,992 --> 00:37:09,450
and we take a hyperrectangle that's

764
00:37:09,450 --> 00:37:11,070
aligned with the other axis.

765
00:37:11,070 --> 00:37:14,210
And we realize that only in the place

766
00:37:14,210 --> 00:37:17,010
where they're both kind of on, if that makes sense, where they

767
00:37:17,010 --> 00:37:20,170
cross each other, the height of the thing, if we just add them,

768
00:37:20,170 --> 00:37:21,330
is 2.

769
00:37:21,330 --> 00:37:24,690
And then the height of the thing where only one of them on is 1.

770
00:37:24,690 --> 00:37:26,670
So do you see that's this picture?

771
00:37:26,670 --> 00:37:30,530
We add them and we get this plus-shaped surface,

772
00:37:30,530 --> 00:37:32,810
where in the very middle, it's height

773
00:37:32,810 --> 00:37:37,210
2 and in the other places, it's either height 1 or height 0.

774
00:37:37,210 --> 00:37:39,850
And so the observation is that we can then

775
00:37:39,850 --> 00:37:43,070
add these rectangles in one dimension,

776
00:37:43,070 --> 00:37:48,010
subtract 1, in this case, or in general d minus 1

777
00:37:48,010 --> 00:37:49,550
and bring the surface down.

778
00:37:49,550 --> 00:37:53,010
And then the only place which is kind of coming above the axis

779
00:37:53,010 --> 00:37:55,835
is the place where they're all intersecting.

780
00:37:55,835 --> 00:37:57,460
And then we're just going to threshold.

781
00:37:57,460 --> 00:37:59,860
We're just going to apply ReLU on top of that.

782
00:37:59,860 --> 00:38:04,040
And it's just going to pick out the place where they're all on.

783
00:38:04,040 --> 00:38:06,680
Does that make sense?

784
00:38:06,680 --> 00:38:10,743
So they'll only all be on where they all intersect.

785
00:38:10,743 --> 00:38:13,160
And then we just shift the whole thing down and threshold,

786
00:38:13,160 --> 00:38:15,080
just to slice off the top.

787
00:38:15,080 --> 00:38:17,780
And it's going to give us that little cube by just slicing off.

788
00:38:17,780 --> 00:38:20,760
And we can do all of that just by adding and doing a ReLU.

789
00:38:20,760 --> 00:38:22,060
So that's the logic.

790
00:38:24,760 --> 00:38:29,840
And now we're ready to assemble all the pieces.

791
00:38:29,840 --> 00:38:34,680
So the top thing is the two-layer ReLU network that

792
00:38:34,680 --> 00:38:36,920
can approximate a rectangle.

793
00:38:36,920 --> 00:38:42,460
We can then sum these things over dimensions,

794
00:38:42,460 --> 00:38:46,860
subtract d minus 1 like we just said, and then take a ReLU.

795
00:38:46,860 --> 00:38:50,240
And this will now give us the hyperrectangle that we always

796
00:38:50,240 --> 00:38:52,620
wanted with this constant c.

797
00:38:55,465 --> 00:38:57,590
c is the thing that we're going to take to infinity

798
00:38:57,590 --> 00:39:00,730
to make the slopes slopier.

799
00:39:04,830 --> 00:39:07,350
And then finally, we're just going

800
00:39:07,350 --> 00:39:12,786
to take linear combination of these things

801
00:39:12,786 --> 00:39:14,272
with constants alpha i, which are

802
00:39:14,272 --> 00:39:16,230
going to set the height of each hyperrectangle.

803
00:39:16,230 --> 00:39:20,830
That corresponds to adding one more layer

804
00:39:20,830 --> 00:39:22,090
on top of our network.

805
00:39:22,090 --> 00:39:25,510
So you can see that in this construction,

806
00:39:25,510 --> 00:39:28,710
there's a ReLU here and there's a ReLU here.

807
00:39:28,710 --> 00:39:30,590
So it really is a three-layer network,

808
00:39:30,590 --> 00:39:32,750
by the definition that we gave.

809
00:39:32,750 --> 00:39:35,550
And then the final step is just to let this constant c grow

810
00:39:35,550 --> 00:39:37,750
really large so that we really do

811
00:39:37,750 --> 00:39:40,350
approximate the hyperrectangles.

812
00:39:40,350 --> 00:39:42,930
And we can approximate our arbitrary surface.

813
00:39:45,550 --> 00:39:48,430
Let's just recap where we got to.

814
00:39:48,430 --> 00:39:50,970
We were going to try to prove this theorem.

815
00:39:53,580 --> 00:39:55,220
And the general idea was we're going

816
00:39:55,220 --> 00:39:56,970
to approximate these-- you could call them

817
00:39:56,970 --> 00:39:59,060
bumps or these hyperrectangle functions-- we're

818
00:39:59,060 --> 00:40:01,680
going to use ReLU to approximate those things.

819
00:40:01,680 --> 00:40:03,340
And then the final step is going to be

820
00:40:03,340 --> 00:40:04,980
to take a linear combination.

821
00:40:07,780 --> 00:40:10,280
So I'm going to dwell here a little longer.

822
00:40:10,280 --> 00:40:13,820
The intention of this slide was to think about some

823
00:40:13,820 --> 00:40:16,620
of the limitations of what we just did.

824
00:40:16,620 --> 00:40:20,260
I feel like some of them you already raised like throughout.

825
00:40:20,260 --> 00:40:25,220
But does anyone want to discuss anything or point out?

826
00:40:25,220 --> 00:40:26,280
Oh, yeah, go ahead.

827
00:40:26,280 --> 00:40:28,380
AUDIENCE: So the two different layers of ReLUs

828
00:40:28,380 --> 00:40:31,820
do very different functions over that?

829
00:40:36,122 --> 00:40:37,580
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, one of them

830
00:40:37,580 --> 00:40:40,860
is about approximating the rectangle

831
00:40:40,860 --> 00:40:42,720
to get the slopes of the sides.

832
00:40:42,720 --> 00:40:44,820
And the other ReLU is more about slicing off

833
00:40:44,820 --> 00:40:47,180
the top of the hyperrectangle.

834
00:40:47,180 --> 00:40:48,060
AUDIENCE: OK, great.

835
00:40:50,692 --> 00:40:51,650
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah?

836
00:40:51,650 --> 00:40:53,192
AUDIENCE: Has there been any research

837
00:40:53,192 --> 00:40:56,650
in determining if ReLU networks actually do something?

838
00:40:56,650 --> 00:40:57,970
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Well, yeah.

839
00:40:57,970 --> 00:40:59,637
Let's talk about that on the next slide,

840
00:40:59,637 --> 00:41:01,290
because I'm pretty doubtful.

841
00:41:01,290 --> 00:41:04,070
It seems like quite a toy construction-- not a toy,

842
00:41:04,070 --> 00:41:06,830
but it has a very deliberate intention,

843
00:41:06,830 --> 00:41:09,630
which is proving this approximation thing.

844
00:41:09,630 --> 00:41:12,392
And a lot of the steps seem a bit kind of fishy,

845
00:41:12,392 --> 00:41:13,850
in terms of would this be something

846
00:41:13,850 --> 00:41:15,350
that a neural network actually does,

847
00:41:15,350 --> 00:41:18,433
like letting the constant c tend to infinity?

848
00:41:18,433 --> 00:41:20,350
Usually you don't want the weights to blow up.

849
00:41:20,350 --> 00:41:22,142
That would be a sign that something's going

850
00:41:22,142 --> 00:41:24,410
wrong in your neural network.

851
00:41:24,410 --> 00:41:27,330
So I wanted to list here a couple of these.

852
00:41:27,330 --> 00:41:32,730
"c tends to infinity" seems a bit questionable.

853
00:41:32,730 --> 00:41:33,290
Yeah?

854
00:41:33,290 --> 00:41:37,010
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] going through the derivation

855
00:41:37,010 --> 00:41:38,110
[INAUDIBLE].

856
00:41:38,110 --> 00:41:40,970
So a few slides back, you set f of x

857
00:41:40,970 --> 00:41:43,293
equal to the sum of all these rectangles?

858
00:41:43,293 --> 00:41:44,210
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

859
00:41:44,210 --> 00:41:46,430
AUDIENCE: But also from my understanding,

860
00:41:46,430 --> 00:41:48,450
at this close approximate g.

861
00:41:48,450 --> 00:41:52,320
And so I was confused how the sum of the rectangles

862
00:41:52,320 --> 00:41:56,105
is associated with this [INAUDIBLE].

863
00:41:56,105 --> 00:41:56,980
Does that make sense?

864
00:41:56,980 --> 00:41:57,897
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

865
00:41:57,897 --> 00:41:59,740
It's because of the use of these.

866
00:42:02,500 --> 00:42:04,840
We're thinking about it symbolically,

867
00:42:04,840 --> 00:42:07,560
as being a weighted sum of indicator functions.

868
00:42:07,560 --> 00:42:09,600
So you think of the rectangle as being

869
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:13,880
a function, which is 1 if the input lies in this interval,

870
00:42:13,880 --> 00:42:15,640
and 0 otherwise.

871
00:42:15,640 --> 00:42:17,960
AUDIENCE: I see.

872
00:42:17,960 --> 00:42:21,820
So I thought it said i and not the width of an indicator.

873
00:42:21,820 --> 00:42:23,072
That clarifies it.

874
00:42:23,072 --> 00:42:25,280
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, sorry, I didn't spell it out.

875
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:28,440
There's an annotated version of the slides on the website where

876
00:42:28,440 --> 00:42:30,180
it's a bit more spelled out.

877
00:42:30,180 --> 00:42:33,140
But exactly, it's not an interval there.

878
00:42:33,140 --> 00:42:34,220
It's an indicator.

879
00:42:34,220 --> 00:42:36,240
AUDIENCE: And so if it's an indicator,

880
00:42:36,240 --> 00:42:42,002
then we're summing the height at each point in the rectangle?

881
00:42:42,002 --> 00:42:42,960
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

882
00:42:42,960 --> 00:42:46,760
The way to think about it is, for a given input,

883
00:42:46,760 --> 00:42:49,790
let's say this is our input, only this indicator

884
00:42:49,790 --> 00:42:51,250
is triggered.

885
00:42:51,250 --> 00:42:53,010
And then it gets scaled by its height.

886
00:42:53,010 --> 00:42:55,510
So there's a sum of terms, but only one of them

887
00:42:55,510 --> 00:42:57,383
will ever be active.

888
00:42:57,383 --> 00:42:58,050
AUDIENCE: I see.

889
00:42:58,050 --> 00:43:00,450
That makes sense.

890
00:43:00,450 --> 00:43:02,610
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: OK, one last question, please.

891
00:43:02,610 --> 00:43:05,110
AUDIENCE: How did you get the 4D [INAUDIBLE]?

892
00:43:05,110 --> 00:43:09,790
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, the 4D is about counting the number

893
00:43:09,790 --> 00:43:13,090
of neurons in this construction.

894
00:43:13,090 --> 00:43:17,410
And the point is that in the top purple bit, the rectangle,

895
00:43:17,410 --> 00:43:19,430
there's four neurons.

896
00:43:19,430 --> 00:43:23,930
Then, in the hyperrectangle, I'm summing over d of those terms,

897
00:43:23,930 --> 00:43:26,590
so it gets 4 multiplied by d.

898
00:43:26,590 --> 00:43:30,130
And then so each hyper rectangle needs 4d neurons.

899
00:43:30,130 --> 00:43:32,390
And then I have the final term, which

900
00:43:32,390 --> 00:43:34,370
is the number of hyperrectangles I need.

901
00:43:34,370 --> 00:43:36,270
So that's why it's 4d.

902
00:43:36,270 --> 00:43:38,910
Yeah, I hope it's right because I just tried to work out

903
00:43:38,910 --> 00:43:40,570
the constant yesterday.

904
00:43:40,570 --> 00:43:42,510
But let's see.

905
00:43:42,510 --> 00:43:45,510
OK, let's just go ahead.

906
00:43:45,510 --> 00:43:48,400
So I wanted to think of it.

907
00:43:48,400 --> 00:43:50,820
Is this really realistic of what would actually

908
00:43:50,820 --> 00:43:51,717
happen in training?

909
00:43:51,717 --> 00:43:53,300
One of the questions is, if we're just

910
00:43:53,300 --> 00:43:55,140
trying to say that a ReLU thing is going

911
00:43:55,140 --> 00:43:57,620
to approximate a rectangle, then why wouldn't we

912
00:43:57,620 --> 00:44:00,980
just use a rectangle as our basis function to begin with?

913
00:44:00,980 --> 00:44:05,300
Because there's something kind of circuitous about not just

914
00:44:05,300 --> 00:44:06,200
doing that.

915
00:44:06,200 --> 00:44:08,260
So let's just pretend that we've created

916
00:44:08,260 --> 00:44:11,260
a basis of these rectangles and that someone gave us

917
00:44:11,260 --> 00:44:14,600
some data, some x's and y's, and we're going to fit it.

918
00:44:14,600 --> 00:44:16,940
And just think about how the training would go.

919
00:44:16,940 --> 00:44:19,220
And what I'm claiming is basically,

920
00:44:19,220 --> 00:44:21,140
every time we see this data point,

921
00:44:21,140 --> 00:44:24,980
this rectangle would get drawn down towards it.

922
00:44:24,980 --> 00:44:27,562
And every time we see this one, this one would.

923
00:44:27,562 --> 00:44:29,520
And every time we see this one, this one would.

924
00:44:29,520 --> 00:44:31,600
And every time we see this one, we get this one.

925
00:44:31,600 --> 00:44:36,060
But there's all these other ones which are never actually going

926
00:44:36,060 --> 00:44:37,340
to move.

927
00:44:37,340 --> 00:44:42,180
And so it's really kind of unnatural.

928
00:44:42,180 --> 00:44:44,780
Visually, the obvious way to approximate this data

929
00:44:44,780 --> 00:44:46,550
is just to draw a line through it,

930
00:44:46,550 --> 00:44:49,125
not to take rectangles at each point

931
00:44:49,125 --> 00:44:50,750
and move them up to the height of the--

932
00:44:50,750 --> 00:44:52,790
So there's something unnatural about it.

933
00:44:52,790 --> 00:44:55,170
But this is the construction that we

934
00:44:55,170 --> 00:44:57,693
use to prove the theorem.

935
00:44:57,693 --> 00:44:59,610
In particular, the thing I'm pointing out here

936
00:44:59,610 --> 00:45:03,223
is that it seems like if you actually fit data this way,

937
00:45:03,223 --> 00:45:05,389
the training performance is going to be really good,

938
00:45:05,389 --> 00:45:06,931
but the generalization performance is

939
00:45:06,931 --> 00:45:07,890
going to be really bad.

940
00:45:07,890 --> 00:45:13,350
Because you're just fitting the data on tiny little strips,

941
00:45:13,350 --> 00:45:15,550
which doesn't seem amazing.

942
00:45:19,450 --> 00:45:22,330
OK, just to reassess where we're up to,

943
00:45:22,330 --> 00:45:24,930
the goal was just to introduce you to one

944
00:45:24,930 --> 00:45:28,890
particular function approximation result,

945
00:45:28,890 --> 00:45:29,990
of which there are many.

946
00:45:29,990 --> 00:45:31,750
There are many papers on this topic.

947
00:45:31,750 --> 00:45:33,970
And there are some classic papers on this topic.

948
00:45:33,970 --> 00:45:37,490
So one is called Barron's theorem, which uses something

949
00:45:37,490 --> 00:45:40,530
about the Fourier representation of the function

950
00:45:40,530 --> 00:45:42,050
that you're trying to fit.

951
00:45:42,050 --> 00:45:46,160
And there's a classic result. So our construction there

952
00:45:46,160 --> 00:45:47,800
was using three layers.

953
00:45:47,800 --> 00:45:50,193
There's a classic result which uses two layers

954
00:45:50,193 --> 00:45:52,360
to do universal function approximation, and actually

955
00:45:52,360 --> 00:45:56,360
a more powerful result, from my understanding.

956
00:45:56,360 --> 00:46:00,517
And it uses something called the Stone-Weierstrass theorem, which

957
00:46:00,517 --> 00:46:01,600
I thought was interesting.

958
00:46:01,600 --> 00:46:04,120
Because Weierstrass was this mathematician who,

959
00:46:04,120 --> 00:46:07,780
I think he was alive in the 1800s, but I'm not 100% sure.

960
00:46:07,780 --> 00:46:09,960
But he came up with that fractal curve

961
00:46:09,960 --> 00:46:13,020
and was thinking about these analysis questions.

962
00:46:13,020 --> 00:46:16,320
But he was actually, seemingly thinking about approximation

963
00:46:16,320 --> 00:46:16,860
as well.

964
00:46:16,860 --> 00:46:21,760
And he had a theorem, which was like, you can use polynomials

965
00:46:21,760 --> 00:46:25,200
to approximate any continuous function or something like this.

966
00:46:25,200 --> 00:46:26,600
And it's just kind of interesting

967
00:46:26,600 --> 00:46:29,520
that this branch of math is related to a very

968
00:46:29,520 --> 00:46:32,640
classical branch of math.

969
00:46:32,640 --> 00:46:35,480
But that's all I have to say.

970
00:46:35,480 --> 00:46:38,360
So on this slide, I want to ask--

971
00:46:38,360 --> 00:46:42,270
is the thing that we just spent 45 minutes talking about,

972
00:46:42,270 --> 00:46:44,710
is it actually important?

973
00:46:44,710 --> 00:46:48,230
And here I'm just abbreviating UFA to be universal function

974
00:46:48,230 --> 00:46:49,090
approximation.

975
00:46:49,090 --> 00:46:50,070
Uh-huh?

976
00:46:50,070 --> 00:46:51,590
AUDIENCE: Where does this lie--

977
00:46:51,590 --> 00:46:54,430
I guess the universal function approximation theorem--

978
00:46:54,430 --> 00:46:58,910
lie in accordance with using Taylor's approximation--

979
00:46:58,910 --> 00:47:00,670
or Taylor's expansion of a function

980
00:47:00,670 --> 00:47:03,750
and fitting a neural network using polynomials

981
00:47:03,750 --> 00:47:04,813
for [INAUDIBLE] function?

982
00:47:04,813 --> 00:47:06,230
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Well, that would

983
00:47:06,230 --> 00:47:09,830
be another way of just fitting the Taylor expansion up

984
00:47:09,830 --> 00:47:13,190
to some degree, which is a bit like that Weierstrass

985
00:47:13,190 --> 00:47:17,050
thing of using polynomials to fit a continuous function.

986
00:47:17,050 --> 00:47:19,030
It's like a valid mathematical way

987
00:47:19,030 --> 00:47:22,310
to approximate a function, using a Taylor series.

988
00:47:22,310 --> 00:47:24,790
But it's just not what we're doing in deep learning.

989
00:47:24,790 --> 00:47:26,490
In deep learning, we take a neural net.

990
00:47:26,490 --> 00:47:29,710
It's just different.

991
00:47:29,710 --> 00:47:32,370
Thinking about that question more, I think is great.

992
00:47:32,370 --> 00:47:37,030
I don't have any really great comments.

993
00:47:37,030 --> 00:47:39,052
Yeah, putting all of these different ways

994
00:47:39,052 --> 00:47:41,260
of thinking about things and trying to reconcile them

995
00:47:41,260 --> 00:47:45,420
is like a really productive thing to try to do.

996
00:47:45,420 --> 00:47:48,460
So the point of here was to say, is universal function

997
00:47:48,460 --> 00:47:49,680
approximation sufficient?

998
00:47:49,680 --> 00:47:52,557
Is it like, OK, that was lecture 3--

999
00:47:52,557 --> 00:47:55,140
now for the rest of the class, we'll just do applications now,

1000
00:47:55,140 --> 00:47:57,240
because we understand the theory of deep learning?

1001
00:47:57,240 --> 00:47:59,540
Probably not.

1002
00:47:59,540 --> 00:48:01,580
So I wanted to give examples of other things

1003
00:48:01,580 --> 00:48:04,080
which are universal function approximations.

1004
00:48:04,080 --> 00:48:08,900
So other examples would be polynomials.

1005
00:48:08,900 --> 00:48:11,300
Can anyone think of another example of something

1006
00:48:11,300 --> 00:48:13,980
which seems like it's probably also a universal function

1007
00:48:13,980 --> 00:48:15,000
approximator?

1008
00:48:18,520 --> 00:48:19,520
AUDIENCE: Random search.

1009
00:48:19,520 --> 00:48:20,658
[INAUDIBLE]

1010
00:48:20,658 --> 00:48:21,700
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Random?

1011
00:48:21,700 --> 00:48:25,340
Yeah, how would you parameterize the space of functions?

1012
00:48:25,340 --> 00:48:28,040
AUDIENCE: So in the previous example

1013
00:48:28,040 --> 00:48:30,180
when we had the hypercube, we can just again

1014
00:48:30,180 --> 00:48:32,660
segment it into N locations.

1015
00:48:32,660 --> 00:48:35,980
And then when we train, we just get stuck at whatever point

1016
00:48:35,980 --> 00:48:37,412
we got right.

1017
00:48:37,412 --> 00:48:38,370
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

1018
00:48:38,370 --> 00:48:40,252
So here we're not really doing training.

1019
00:48:40,252 --> 00:48:42,710
We just want to think about what is the space of functions.

1020
00:48:42,710 --> 00:48:44,710
So the space of functions you talked about there

1021
00:48:44,710 --> 00:48:48,230
is linear combinations of these hyperrectangle bumps,

1022
00:48:48,230 --> 00:48:49,603
which that's valid.

1023
00:48:49,603 --> 00:48:51,770
It's a bit wordy, so I'm not going to write it down,

1024
00:48:51,770 --> 00:48:53,530
but that's valid.

1025
00:48:53,530 --> 00:48:55,090
The other one I wanted to point out

1026
00:48:55,090 --> 00:49:00,810
is something like the space of Python programs.

1027
00:49:00,810 --> 00:49:02,910
So programming languages are, in a sense,

1028
00:49:02,910 --> 00:49:04,750
a universal function approximator.

1029
00:49:04,750 --> 00:49:07,770
Does that mean that we've solved machine

1030
00:49:07,770 --> 00:49:09,690
learning because program--

1031
00:49:09,690 --> 00:49:12,090
it's like many, many things are universal function

1032
00:49:12,090 --> 00:49:13,630
approximators.

1033
00:49:13,630 --> 00:49:15,022
It's just a piece of the puzzle.

1034
00:49:15,022 --> 00:49:16,730
That's the thing I'm trying to point out.

1035
00:49:16,730 --> 00:49:18,310
Just because you've installed Python,

1036
00:49:18,310 --> 00:49:20,710
you can't start doing machine learning.

1037
00:49:20,710 --> 00:49:22,270
You need something else.

1038
00:49:26,010 --> 00:49:29,750
And is it necessary for machine learning to work?

1039
00:49:29,750 --> 00:49:31,650
So if your machine learning model

1040
00:49:31,650 --> 00:49:33,710
is not a universal function approximator,

1041
00:49:33,710 --> 00:49:34,830
should you be worried?

1042
00:49:38,960 --> 00:49:40,120
Someone's saying no.

1043
00:49:40,120 --> 00:49:44,400
Yeah, I sort of agree, but I'm honestly not sure.

1044
00:49:44,400 --> 00:49:47,220
Yeah, I think it's just an interesting question to ask.

1045
00:49:47,220 --> 00:49:48,380
Do we need it to be?

1046
00:49:48,380 --> 00:49:49,583
My guess is not.

1047
00:49:49,583 --> 00:49:51,000
My guess is probably we don't need

1048
00:49:51,000 --> 00:49:52,560
to have a universal function approximator

1049
00:49:52,560 --> 00:49:53,540
to do machine learning.

1050
00:49:53,540 --> 00:49:59,080
But I think it's a little unclear how to answer that.

1051
00:49:59,080 --> 00:50:06,040
So now I want to move on to the second half of the lecture,

1052
00:50:06,040 --> 00:50:09,080
which is going to be thinking more

1053
00:50:09,080 --> 00:50:13,160
about that question of width versus depth.

1054
00:50:13,160 --> 00:50:15,880
So again, would you rather scale the width of your model

1055
00:50:15,880 --> 00:50:17,260
or scale the depth?

1056
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:23,160
And again, we've just tried to prove in some sense

1057
00:50:23,160 --> 00:50:26,840
that with a very small depth network, like depth 3,

1058
00:50:26,840 --> 00:50:28,740
we can fit any function.

1059
00:50:28,740 --> 00:50:31,700
So that's coming back to this question of,

1060
00:50:31,700 --> 00:50:35,040
if three layers is enough, why would we ever want

1061
00:50:35,040 --> 00:50:37,490
to have a deep network?

1062
00:50:37,490 --> 00:50:41,430
Does anyone have a thought?

1063
00:50:41,430 --> 00:50:42,250
What do you think?

1064
00:50:42,250 --> 00:50:43,430
Yeah?

1065
00:50:43,430 --> 00:50:47,270
AUDIENCE: I'm guessing because in the terms of nailing

1066
00:50:47,270 --> 00:50:52,310
the network and stuff, you can approximate, I'm guessing,

1067
00:50:52,310 --> 00:50:56,350
some sort of arbitrary nonlinearity, because you have

1068
00:50:56,350 --> 00:50:58,570
pointwise nonlinearities in each of the neurons,

1069
00:50:58,570 --> 00:50:59,930
you have function compositions.

1070
00:50:59,930 --> 00:51:05,190
So those would help you to do a complex mapping.

1071
00:51:05,190 --> 00:51:09,150
But width would be going back to the universal approximation

1072
00:51:09,150 --> 00:51:10,150
theorem.

1073
00:51:10,150 --> 00:51:11,150
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Right.

1074
00:51:11,150 --> 00:51:11,710
That's great.

1075
00:51:11,710 --> 00:51:13,250
Let me just summarize.

1076
00:51:13,250 --> 00:51:13,750
I forgot.

1077
00:51:13,750 --> 00:51:16,410
I'm always supposed to summarize questions and comments,

1078
00:51:16,410 --> 00:51:17,410
but I forgot about that.

1079
00:51:17,410 --> 00:51:21,910
But basically, if we layer lots of layers,

1080
00:51:21,910 --> 00:51:24,250
then we have a kind of compound effect.

1081
00:51:24,250 --> 00:51:26,010
We have more nonlinearities.

1082
00:51:26,010 --> 00:51:27,510
It seems like maybe that can give us

1083
00:51:27,510 --> 00:51:31,790
a richer space of functions through compositionality.

1084
00:51:31,790 --> 00:51:33,010
Yeah, that's a great point.

1085
00:51:33,010 --> 00:51:34,520
Sorry, what was your--

1086
00:51:34,520 --> 00:51:36,820
AUDIENCE: Maybe on surface what I think we can have is

1087
00:51:36,820 --> 00:51:38,660
we can put on lines and dots.

1088
00:51:38,660 --> 00:51:43,860
The width might be faster or take less memory.

1089
00:51:43,860 --> 00:51:47,155
But if you don't, then that might be good.

1090
00:51:47,155 --> 00:51:48,780
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: This is a great point

1091
00:51:48,780 --> 00:51:50,155
that, if you think about building

1092
00:51:50,155 --> 00:51:53,760
a machine learning system, width can be parallelized.

1093
00:51:53,760 --> 00:51:56,660
It makes better use of parallel hardware.

1094
00:51:56,660 --> 00:51:59,940
So from a systems perspective, if you can get away

1095
00:51:59,940 --> 00:52:02,100
with a shallow network that's very wide,

1096
00:52:02,100 --> 00:52:06,620
you would really love that, because you can parallelize it.

1097
00:52:06,620 --> 00:52:10,080
And that's illustrative that there's different constraints.

1098
00:52:10,080 --> 00:52:13,900
There's the, can I represent complex and interesting

1099
00:52:13,900 --> 00:52:14,458
functions?

1100
00:52:14,458 --> 00:52:16,500
And then there's the computational constraint of,

1101
00:52:16,500 --> 00:52:18,200
how efficient is this to run?

1102
00:52:18,200 --> 00:52:21,280
There's other constraints of that sort.

1103
00:52:24,380 --> 00:52:27,300
So on this slide, I wanted to just

1104
00:52:27,300 --> 00:52:30,820
think, what are the advantages of width?

1105
00:52:30,820 --> 00:52:32,830
So one is parallelism.

1106
00:52:35,890 --> 00:52:40,090
One is a three-layer neural net is a universal function

1107
00:52:40,090 --> 00:52:43,230
approximator, or maybe even two layers is enough.

1108
00:52:47,970 --> 00:52:50,670
Can anyone think of any other advantages of width?

1109
00:52:56,335 --> 00:52:58,210
AUDIENCE: You get the feature representations

1110
00:52:58,210 --> 00:53:00,970
that are nicer and more easily interpretable.

1111
00:53:00,970 --> 00:53:05,390
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Mm-hmm, easier to interpret.

1112
00:53:05,390 --> 00:53:06,890
I'm trying to remember if there were

1113
00:53:06,890 --> 00:53:08,270
any others that I thought of.

1114
00:53:08,270 --> 00:53:09,370
Does anyone else have any?

1115
00:53:13,050 --> 00:53:17,568
AUDIENCE: It appears [INAUDIBLE] gradients.

1116
00:53:17,568 --> 00:53:20,110
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, easier to train, that's a good point.

1117
00:53:23,730 --> 00:53:25,110
Easier to optimize.

1118
00:53:29,590 --> 00:53:33,040
Yeah, because whenever you do compound anything,

1119
00:53:33,040 --> 00:53:35,540
it is very liable to get out of control.

1120
00:53:35,540 --> 00:53:38,160
So making your network too deep without being

1121
00:53:38,160 --> 00:53:40,480
careful about how you do that can actually

1122
00:53:40,480 --> 00:53:42,040
just break the training.

1123
00:53:42,040 --> 00:53:44,760
So if you just take a vanilla multilayer perceptron

1124
00:53:44,760 --> 00:53:47,860
and make it 50 layers deep and just try to train it,

1125
00:53:47,860 --> 00:53:53,320
you'll see it's very difficult to get it to train.

1126
00:53:53,320 --> 00:53:54,200
OK.

1127
00:53:54,200 --> 00:53:57,840
I can't think of any others, unless anyone else had.

1128
00:53:57,840 --> 00:54:02,200
So the point of this slide was to say, well, OK, obviously wide

1129
00:54:02,200 --> 00:54:04,920
is better than deep, right?

1130
00:54:04,920 --> 00:54:06,060
That's obvious now.

1131
00:54:06,060 --> 00:54:09,660
But then the next slide is supposed to say, is it?

1132
00:54:14,120 --> 00:54:17,395
Because now in the last part of the lecture

1133
00:54:17,395 --> 00:54:19,020
or almost the last part of the lecture,

1134
00:54:19,020 --> 00:54:20,812
we're going to think about something called

1135
00:54:20,812 --> 00:54:25,320
depth separation results, which is another potentially quite

1136
00:54:25,320 --> 00:54:30,750
stylized theoretical result, but it is quite interesting.

1137
00:54:30,750 --> 00:54:34,750
So remember that the universal function approximation results

1138
00:54:34,750 --> 00:54:37,310
suggested that we need to make our network exponentially

1139
00:54:37,310 --> 00:54:40,150
wide in order to be able to approximate everything

1140
00:54:40,150 --> 00:54:41,250
that we want to.

1141
00:54:41,250 --> 00:54:43,310
And that seems bad.

1142
00:54:43,310 --> 00:54:45,150
So the point of a depth separation result

1143
00:54:45,150 --> 00:54:48,070
is to say that there's actually a deep network that

1144
00:54:48,070 --> 00:54:52,870
can represent a particular function quite efficiently.

1145
00:54:52,870 --> 00:54:54,870
And if you tried to represent the same function

1146
00:54:54,870 --> 00:54:58,150
with a three-layer network or a shallower network,

1147
00:54:58,150 --> 00:55:02,470
you would need exponentially more neurons to do that.

1148
00:55:02,470 --> 00:55:07,910
So the shape of a depth separation result,

1149
00:55:07,910 --> 00:55:12,610
I'm just trying to break it up into stages, is first of all,

1150
00:55:12,610 --> 00:55:14,690
we pick a property of a function.

1151
00:55:17,430 --> 00:55:19,750
And the example we're going to think of

1152
00:55:19,750 --> 00:55:21,910
is the number of linear regions.

1153
00:55:24,870 --> 00:55:27,220
We think of the function as being piecewise linear.

1154
00:55:27,220 --> 00:55:30,140
And how many linear pieces are there?

1155
00:55:30,140 --> 00:55:32,460
And then we construct a deep network

1156
00:55:32,460 --> 00:55:36,980
that has a lot of that property, but is not a particularly

1157
00:55:36,980 --> 00:55:38,360
big network in some sense.

1158
00:55:38,360 --> 00:55:39,900
It doesn't have a lot of neurons.

1159
00:55:39,900 --> 00:55:42,520
And that is a constructive thing.

1160
00:55:42,520 --> 00:55:44,140
We say, here is a neural network which

1161
00:55:44,140 --> 00:55:45,980
has a lot of this property.

1162
00:55:45,980 --> 00:55:48,780
And then we say, now we're going to prove that it's actually

1163
00:55:48,780 --> 00:55:51,900
impossible for a shallow network to have that same property

1164
00:55:51,900 --> 00:55:55,940
unless it has exponentially many neurons or a huge number

1165
00:55:55,940 --> 00:55:57,700
of neurons.

1166
00:55:57,700 --> 00:55:59,440
So that's the shape of this result.

1167
00:55:59,440 --> 00:56:03,020
And again, there's a literature which just proves

1168
00:56:03,020 --> 00:56:05,340
different varieties of this kind of result.

1169
00:56:05,340 --> 00:56:07,560
And that's called depth separation results.

1170
00:56:07,560 --> 00:56:10,640
We're just going to show you one particular example.

1171
00:56:13,580 --> 00:56:16,980
So recall that a piecewise linear function

1172
00:56:16,980 --> 00:56:20,080
is a function where if you break it up into segments,

1173
00:56:20,080 --> 00:56:21,600
each segment is linear.

1174
00:56:21,600 --> 00:56:23,200
And then they kind of stitch together.

1175
00:56:26,610 --> 00:56:29,690
And we'll think about defining the number of kinks

1176
00:56:29,690 --> 00:56:31,730
in such a function to mean--

1177
00:56:34,850 --> 00:56:38,810
a kink is how many of these discontinuities

1178
00:56:38,810 --> 00:56:42,090
are there in the derivative, how many places does

1179
00:56:42,090 --> 00:56:44,610
the derivative suddenly change.

1180
00:56:44,610 --> 00:56:49,290
And so for this function, you can see there's four kinks.

1181
00:56:49,290 --> 00:56:53,210
And that's the property that we're going to pick.

1182
00:56:53,210 --> 00:56:55,130
And we're going to show that there's

1183
00:56:55,130 --> 00:56:57,290
a deep network with not very many neurons

1184
00:56:57,290 --> 00:56:58,453
that has a lot of kinks.

1185
00:56:58,453 --> 00:57:00,870
And if you want to have that many kinks in a wide network,

1186
00:57:00,870 --> 00:57:02,390
you need exponentially many neurons.

1187
00:57:05,782 --> 00:57:07,990
That's the strategy of what we're going to try to do.

1188
00:57:07,990 --> 00:57:11,310
But the first claim is that ReLU networks are piecewise linear.

1189
00:57:11,310 --> 00:57:15,090
So if you have a ReLU network with one input and one output,

1190
00:57:15,090 --> 00:57:17,650
it will look something like this green curve.

1191
00:57:17,650 --> 00:57:22,650
Can anyone tell me why that's the case?

1192
00:57:22,650 --> 00:57:24,500
Or does anyone think it is the case?

1193
00:57:24,500 --> 00:57:26,420
Or does anyone think it's not the case?

1194
00:57:36,120 --> 00:57:36,920
Yeah?

1195
00:57:36,920 --> 00:57:39,560
AUDIENCE: It's because the ReLU is essentially

1196
00:57:39,560 --> 00:57:41,460
a linear function, but with a threshold.

1197
00:57:41,460 --> 00:57:45,040
So when you add them, it creates some kind of shape.

1198
00:57:45,040 --> 00:57:47,400
And the transformation that you did before

1199
00:57:47,400 --> 00:57:51,700
is linear, so it's only going to distort it but in a linear way.

1200
00:57:51,700 --> 00:57:53,400
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Great.

1201
00:57:53,400 --> 00:57:55,760
Yeah, I think with this kind of statement,

1202
00:57:55,760 --> 00:57:57,943
with many sorts of statements-- yeah, exactly.

1203
00:57:57,943 --> 00:57:59,360
But with many sorts of statements,

1204
00:57:59,360 --> 00:58:01,040
we want to break them up into--

1205
00:58:04,200 --> 00:58:05,700
I'm not sure what the right word is.

1206
00:58:05,700 --> 00:58:08,600
But we want to be able to say, if f and g are

1207
00:58:08,600 --> 00:58:12,640
both piecewise linear, then that implies that f composed with g

1208
00:58:12,640 --> 00:58:14,200
is piecewise linear.

1209
00:58:14,200 --> 00:58:16,840
If f and g are both piecewise linear,

1210
00:58:16,840 --> 00:58:20,120
that implies that f plus g is piecewise linear.

1211
00:58:20,120 --> 00:58:23,150
If f is piecewise linear, then alpha

1212
00:58:23,150 --> 00:58:25,050
f is also piecewise linear.

1213
00:58:25,050 --> 00:58:27,670
So you break up the construction of the function

1214
00:58:27,670 --> 00:58:30,710
into a series of combinations, and you

1215
00:58:30,710 --> 00:58:32,310
prove that the property is preserved

1216
00:58:32,310 --> 00:58:35,090
under those combinations.

1217
00:58:35,090 --> 00:58:36,590
And then that and then basically you

1218
00:58:36,590 --> 00:58:42,750
realize that, OK, a ReLU neural network,

1219
00:58:42,750 --> 00:58:44,568
the ReLU function is piecewise linear.

1220
00:58:44,568 --> 00:58:46,610
And then it's just adding and multiplying things.

1221
00:58:46,610 --> 00:58:47,870
So you're always going to get something

1222
00:58:47,870 --> 00:58:49,090
that's piecewise linear.

1223
00:58:51,610 --> 00:58:54,310
So based on that, let's now construct a depth separation

1224
00:58:54,310 --> 00:58:56,750
based on counting the number of kinks.

1225
00:58:56,750 --> 00:59:00,670
So I'll first give you the intuition

1226
00:59:00,670 --> 00:59:04,270
and then write something a bit more formal.

1227
00:59:04,270 --> 00:59:06,590
But first of all, think about the point of view

1228
00:59:06,590 --> 00:59:08,470
of an individual neuron.

1229
00:59:08,470 --> 00:59:14,030
And we're going to add one extra input neuron.

1230
00:59:14,030 --> 00:59:17,510
And symbolically, then we think about feeding a function,

1231
00:59:17,510 --> 00:59:21,320
like a one-dimensional function, as inputs.

1232
00:59:21,320 --> 00:59:23,780
So we're going to think of the output of this neuron, y

1233
00:59:23,780 --> 00:59:27,940
of x, as just being the summation of scalar multiples

1234
00:59:27,940 --> 00:59:31,820
of the input functions.

1235
00:59:31,820 --> 00:59:37,340
The observation of this slide is that if we add functions,

1236
00:59:37,340 --> 00:59:41,060
at most, we add the number of kinks.

1237
00:59:41,060 --> 00:59:44,020
And I'm just going to try to persuade you that that's

1238
00:59:44,020 --> 00:59:45,860
the case with a picture.

1239
00:59:45,860 --> 00:59:47,860
So here we're thinking of, the green function

1240
00:59:47,860 --> 00:59:49,580
is our first function.

1241
00:59:49,580 --> 00:59:52,260
And the blue function is our second function.

1242
00:59:52,260 --> 00:59:54,220
And then the purple function shows the result

1243
00:59:54,220 --> 00:59:55,820
of adding them together.

1244
00:59:55,820 --> 01:00:00,540
And notice that the green function has one kink.

1245
01:00:00,540 --> 01:00:03,500
The blue function has two kinks.

1246
01:00:03,500 --> 01:00:05,620
And then the purple function, which is their sum,

1247
01:00:05,620 --> 01:00:07,140
has three kinks.

1248
01:00:07,140 --> 01:00:09,180
And nothing more, you can't have more than that,

1249
01:00:09,180 --> 01:00:12,680
because you only can have kinks at the places

1250
01:00:12,680 --> 01:00:14,680
where the functions that you're adding had them.

1251
01:00:14,680 --> 01:00:17,320
So this is how we think about adding functions.

1252
01:00:17,320 --> 01:00:19,490
We can at most add the number of kicks.

1253
01:00:19,490 --> 01:00:21,650
But potentially the kicks could happen

1254
01:00:21,650 --> 01:00:24,355
in the same place, in which case, it

1255
01:00:24,355 --> 01:00:26,730
would be less than adding, because that wouldn't give you

1256
01:00:26,730 --> 01:00:27,750
an extra one.

1257
01:00:27,750 --> 01:00:29,890
But generally, if they're generally spaced out,

1258
01:00:29,890 --> 01:00:31,970
then they would add.

1259
01:00:31,970 --> 01:00:36,570
Next intuition, the effect of applying ReLU.

1260
01:00:36,570 --> 01:00:39,810
So we think now that we have a function f.

1261
01:00:39,810 --> 01:00:42,010
And then we just take ReLU of f.

1262
01:00:42,010 --> 01:00:46,670
So we think that the output is ReLU of f of x, basically.

1263
01:00:46,670 --> 01:00:48,810
So y of x is ReLU of f of x.

1264
01:00:48,810 --> 01:00:53,610
And now the claim is that if we apply ReLU, at most,

1265
01:00:53,610 --> 01:00:56,950
we double the number of kinks.

1266
01:00:56,950 --> 01:00:59,170
And so this picture is supposed to illustrate.

1267
01:00:59,170 --> 01:01:02,530
We think about the red function as being f.

1268
01:01:02,530 --> 01:01:08,970
And then if we apply ReLU, we just chop off the positive part.

1269
01:01:08,970 --> 01:01:10,530
And the observation is that if we

1270
01:01:10,530 --> 01:01:14,810
had a linear region, that can at most get split up

1271
01:01:14,810 --> 01:01:17,900
into two linear regions.

1272
01:01:17,900 --> 01:01:21,200
And if you just count on this picture,

1273
01:01:21,200 --> 01:01:24,540
I think you see that the red function had 1, 2, 3, 4,

1274
01:01:24,540 --> 01:01:32,948
5 kinks, whereas the green function has 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,

1275
01:01:32,948 --> 01:01:35,500
7, 8, 9, 9 kinks.

1276
01:01:35,500 --> 01:01:38,020
So you can see it did nearly double in this case.

1277
01:01:43,920 --> 01:01:47,600
So now to be a bit more formal, we're

1278
01:01:47,600 --> 01:01:50,840
going to imagine that we have a deep ReLU

1279
01:01:50,840 --> 01:01:52,520
network with many layers.

1280
01:01:52,520 --> 01:01:57,880
And we're going to examine one layer inside the network.

1281
01:01:57,880 --> 01:02:02,920
And we're going to give a recursive definition of what

1282
01:02:02,920 --> 01:02:04,300
this layer looks like.

1283
01:02:04,300 --> 01:02:07,760
So the function at layer L is ReLU

1284
01:02:07,760 --> 01:02:11,160
of the weight matrix times the function from the previous layer

1285
01:02:11,160 --> 01:02:13,880
plus the bias.

1286
01:02:13,880 --> 01:02:18,500
And this is going to be an n-dimensional vector.

1287
01:02:18,500 --> 01:02:22,800
The weight matrix is going to be an n by n matrix.

1288
01:02:22,800 --> 01:02:25,780
This is going to be an n-dimensional vector.

1289
01:02:25,780 --> 01:02:27,840
And the bias is also an n-dimensional vector.

1290
01:02:27,840 --> 01:02:31,740
So that's the shape of all of these things.

1291
01:02:31,740 --> 01:02:36,500
And then we define this capital let

1292
01:02:36,500 --> 01:02:41,020
KINKS-L to denote the maximum number of kinks over the n

1293
01:02:41,020 --> 01:02:43,120
coordinates of the output.

1294
01:02:43,120 --> 01:02:45,380
So remember, this is an n-dimensional vector.

1295
01:02:45,380 --> 01:02:48,220
Each coordinate could have a certain number of kinks

1296
01:02:48,220 --> 01:02:50,140
in its function, because each coordinate

1297
01:02:50,140 --> 01:02:53,860
is a one-dimensional function.

1298
01:02:53,860 --> 01:02:57,140
And then we define this variable to be the maximum

1299
01:02:57,140 --> 01:02:58,860
over those coordinates.

1300
01:02:58,860 --> 01:03:02,420
And maybe I'm just going to write it as a claim.

1301
01:03:02,420 --> 01:03:08,620
But the claim is that KINKS-L can be no greater than 2 times

1302
01:03:08,620 --> 01:03:15,090
the width times the maximum number of kinks in the input.

1303
01:03:15,090 --> 01:03:17,670
So maybe you could think more about this.

1304
01:03:17,670 --> 01:03:21,150
So the 2 comes from the doubling effect of the ReLU.

1305
01:03:21,150 --> 01:03:25,010
And the n comes from the fact that you're adding n things.

1306
01:03:25,010 --> 01:03:27,010
And then the trick is just to think about taking

1307
01:03:27,010 --> 01:03:29,390
the max over each coordinate.

1308
01:03:32,970 --> 01:03:35,050
And then the other observation is

1309
01:03:35,050 --> 01:03:38,130
that the kinks at the input, the input

1310
01:03:38,130 --> 01:03:42,770
is basically 1, because there's no kinks.

1311
01:03:42,770 --> 01:03:45,790
Does that make sense?

1312
01:03:45,790 --> 01:03:47,050
AUDIENCE: It should be 0.

1313
01:03:47,050 --> 01:03:48,230
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: It should be 0, you're right.

1314
01:03:48,230 --> 01:03:49,250
But it's not going to--

1315
01:03:54,962 --> 01:03:56,670
Let's think about this after the lecture.

1316
01:03:56,670 --> 01:04:02,170
But the point is that each time we can at most multiply by 2n.

1317
01:04:02,170 --> 01:04:08,290
So I'm trying to get the answer that KINKS-L is no greater than

1318
01:04:08,290 --> 01:04:17,740
2n to the power L. OK, well, if KINKS-0 is 0,

1319
01:04:17,740 --> 01:04:19,800
it's still less than or equal to 1.

1320
01:04:19,800 --> 01:04:21,440
[LAUGHTER]

1321
01:04:21,440 --> 01:04:24,620
Yeah, it looks like some accounting problem.

1322
01:04:24,620 --> 01:04:26,380
But you see what the argument is.

1323
01:04:26,380 --> 01:04:27,580
I just need to figure out--

1324
01:04:27,580 --> 01:04:28,080
Yeah?

1325
01:04:28,080 --> 01:04:29,788
AUDIENCE: You could start with one layer.

1326
01:04:29,788 --> 01:04:31,372
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, you could just

1327
01:04:31,372 --> 01:04:33,180
start with the base case being one layer.

1328
01:04:33,180 --> 01:04:34,560
That's a great--

1329
01:04:34,560 --> 01:04:36,800
AUDIENCE: You should make it segmented instead.

1330
01:04:36,800 --> 01:04:40,640
Otherwise, the ReLU adds a kink where there was none.

1331
01:04:40,640 --> 01:04:45,160
Well, 0 to 1 is more [INAUDIBLE].

1332
01:04:45,160 --> 01:04:46,720
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

1333
01:04:46,720 --> 01:04:47,560
OK.

1334
01:04:47,560 --> 01:04:49,500
Let's just talk more about this.

1335
01:04:49,500 --> 01:04:51,440
But I think you get the intuition

1336
01:04:51,440 --> 01:04:52,780
in making this rigorous.

1337
01:04:52,780 --> 01:04:54,322
You just need to think about it a bit

1338
01:04:54,322 --> 01:04:58,120
to work out what the base case should be.

1339
01:04:58,120 --> 01:05:01,440
But I hope that the message gets across.

1340
01:05:01,440 --> 01:05:05,500
So in principle, we showed this statement.

1341
01:05:09,560 --> 01:05:13,310
As a reminder, n is the width, capital L is the depth,

1342
01:05:13,310 --> 01:05:18,890
and KINKS-L should be the maximum over the coordinates.

1343
01:05:22,910 --> 01:05:25,790
And so the thing we wanted to point out

1344
01:05:25,790 --> 01:05:34,530
is that the upper bound grows at most polynomially in width,

1345
01:05:34,530 --> 01:05:37,870
but exponentially in depth.

1346
01:05:37,870 --> 01:05:41,510
So this is suggestive that there's a big benefit,

1347
01:05:41,510 --> 01:05:43,710
if we're trying to maximize the number of kinks,

1348
01:05:43,710 --> 01:05:45,230
in making the network deeper.

1349
01:05:45,230 --> 01:05:46,650
Because every time we add a layer,

1350
01:05:46,650 --> 01:05:50,330
we double things, rather than making it wider.

1351
01:05:50,330 --> 01:05:53,550
So if our goal is to be able to approximate functions

1352
01:05:53,550 --> 01:05:56,990
with many kinks, then it's better

1353
01:05:56,990 --> 01:06:01,750
to make the network deeper rather than wider.

1354
01:06:01,750 --> 01:06:05,530
Does anyone have an objection to what we've shown?

1355
01:06:05,530 --> 01:06:09,360
Because it is just an upper bound.

1356
01:06:09,360 --> 01:06:10,860
So the objection that you could have

1357
01:06:10,860 --> 01:06:12,880
is like, that's just an upper bound.

1358
01:06:12,880 --> 01:06:14,740
Maybe it's never attained.

1359
01:06:14,740 --> 01:06:17,242
Maybe in practice, when you actually build a very deep ReLU

1360
01:06:17,242 --> 01:06:18,700
network, it's very difficult to get

1361
01:06:18,700 --> 01:06:20,900
there to be a lot of kinks in the output function.

1362
01:06:20,900 --> 01:06:26,220
So the next step is to provide a construction

1363
01:06:26,220 --> 01:06:28,680
to show you that, no, this can actually happen.

1364
01:06:28,680 --> 01:06:31,280
And again, I'm just going to skip the slide.

1365
01:06:31,280 --> 01:06:36,060
So again, the argument is that this g defined

1366
01:06:36,060 --> 01:06:39,080
as a really small ReLU network in this form,

1367
01:06:39,080 --> 01:06:41,100
if you plot what that actually amounts to,

1368
01:06:41,100 --> 01:06:43,980
it amounts to a triangle.

1369
01:06:43,980 --> 01:06:47,440
And the property of a triangle is that if you iterate it,

1370
01:06:47,440 --> 01:06:50,900
you apply it to itself-- so you do g composed with g,

1371
01:06:50,900 --> 01:06:52,700
you actually get two triangles.

1372
01:06:52,700 --> 01:06:55,020
There should be no gap there.

1373
01:06:55,020 --> 01:06:57,980
Let's see, you get two triangles.

1374
01:06:57,980 --> 01:07:01,780
And if you do g composed with g composed with g,

1375
01:07:01,780 --> 01:07:04,060
you get four triangles.

1376
01:07:04,060 --> 01:07:08,290
So you need to think a little bit to see why that is.

1377
01:07:08,290 --> 01:07:10,510
But actually, I think I didn't need to show you.

1378
01:07:10,510 --> 01:07:14,950
But I was going to demonstrate it on the graphing website.

1379
01:07:14,950 --> 01:07:17,170
But I think hopefully the claim is clear,

1380
01:07:17,170 --> 01:07:21,190
that you can just with ReLUs, make a triangle function.

1381
01:07:21,190 --> 01:07:23,690
And if you think about what happens to the triangle function

1382
01:07:23,690 --> 01:07:26,610
when you compose it with itself, you just

1383
01:07:26,610 --> 01:07:29,810
recursively get double as many triangles each time.

1384
01:07:29,810 --> 01:07:32,730
So the point is that this is an explicit construction

1385
01:07:32,730 --> 01:07:36,370
of a certain ReLU network, where when you compose it with itself

1386
01:07:36,370 --> 01:07:38,770
many, many times, you actually keep

1387
01:07:38,770 --> 01:07:41,890
doubling the number of kinks in the function.

1388
01:07:41,890 --> 01:07:46,790
So it can really happen, at least with this one example.

1389
01:07:49,570 --> 01:07:51,490
And then this is actually just running

1390
01:07:51,490 --> 01:07:54,410
some numbers where I'm like, let's

1391
01:07:54,410 --> 01:07:56,290
suppose that we take that triangle function

1392
01:07:56,290 --> 01:07:59,450
and compose it with itself 500 times,

1393
01:07:59,450 --> 01:08:04,350
that would give us at most 2 to the 500 kinks.

1394
01:08:04,350 --> 01:08:06,660
No, it would actually give us that many kinks.

1395
01:08:06,660 --> 01:08:08,700
It would really would give us that many.

1396
01:08:12,360 --> 01:08:15,260
Actually, each g turns out to have two layers.

1397
01:08:15,260 --> 01:08:18,080
So this would be a 1,000-layer MLP.

1398
01:08:18,080 --> 01:08:22,439
And then if you compute using the bound that we constructed,

1399
01:08:22,439 --> 01:08:25,120
if you took a three-layer MLP, how wide would it

1400
01:08:25,120 --> 01:08:27,899
need to be in order to fit the same function,

1401
01:08:27,899 --> 01:08:30,520
you'd find that it would need to have almost 10

1402
01:08:30,520 --> 01:08:34,520
to the power 50 width in order to fit that same function.

1403
01:08:34,520 --> 01:08:36,540
So this is the point.

1404
01:08:36,540 --> 01:08:38,880
This is the depth separation.

1405
01:08:38,880 --> 01:08:40,640
There's a function, which is not even that

1406
01:08:40,640 --> 01:08:41,819
difficult to write down.

1407
01:08:41,819 --> 01:08:48,779
It's just a triangle or self-composed many, many times.

1408
01:08:48,779 --> 01:08:51,692
And if you wanted to get a shallow network to exactly

1409
01:08:51,692 --> 01:08:53,359
approximate that function, it would need

1410
01:08:53,359 --> 01:08:56,600
to be very, very, very wide.

1411
01:08:56,600 --> 01:08:58,660
This is the reward for coming to the lecture,

1412
01:08:58,660 --> 01:09:02,080
but this is also on the first problem set.

1413
01:09:02,080 --> 01:09:04,910
So just maybe when you're solving the problem set,

1414
01:09:04,910 --> 01:09:08,790
have a think about this.

1415
01:09:08,790 --> 01:09:13,670
OK, I want to now think a bit more broadly.

1416
01:09:13,670 --> 01:09:16,590
What does this not say?

1417
01:09:16,590 --> 01:09:18,910
This depth separation is suggesting

1418
01:09:18,910 --> 01:09:20,430
that there could be a big advantage

1419
01:09:20,430 --> 01:09:22,590
to making things really deep.

1420
01:09:22,590 --> 01:09:24,910
But it's not telling us that deep networks are

1421
01:09:24,910 --> 01:09:27,630
very easy to train, and they might not be.

1422
01:09:27,630 --> 01:09:30,950
So it's not telling us about optimization.

1423
01:09:30,950 --> 01:09:34,612
And it's not telling us about generalization.

1424
01:09:34,612 --> 01:09:37,029
It's not saying that the very deep networks would actually

1425
01:09:37,029 --> 01:09:39,510
perform well on some particular data set

1426
01:09:39,510 --> 01:09:42,350
that we want to apply them to.

1427
01:09:42,350 --> 01:09:45,830
These are really just statements thinking about approximation.

1428
01:09:45,830 --> 01:09:47,770
But this is not the end of the story.

1429
01:09:47,770 --> 01:09:50,050
It's just one piece in this puzzle.

1430
01:09:53,510 --> 01:09:56,390
Again, there's more theorems of this nature.

1431
01:09:56,390 --> 01:09:59,850
And feel free to look more into them.

1432
01:09:59,850 --> 01:10:02,180
The one at the bottom is interesting.

1433
01:10:02,180 --> 01:10:06,800
Because a question you could have is like, OK,

1434
01:10:06,800 --> 01:10:14,140
if increasing depth is so great, maybe I'll just pick width 3

1435
01:10:14,140 --> 01:10:19,700
and just go really deep, and this will work really well.

1436
01:10:19,700 --> 01:10:24,100
But what that paper shows is that if the input space has

1437
01:10:24,100 --> 01:10:27,540
dimension n, you need a width of at least n

1438
01:10:27,540 --> 01:10:30,200
to approximate any function basically.

1439
01:10:30,200 --> 01:10:33,540
So there can be a minimum width that you really need.

1440
01:10:33,540 --> 01:10:35,620
Anyway, there's some further reading,

1441
01:10:35,620 --> 01:10:39,900
in case you're interested to go deeper on this topic.

1442
01:10:39,900 --> 01:10:47,060
So in the last 15 minutes, I guess,

1443
01:10:47,060 --> 01:10:50,620
I want to think about some more practical considerations.

1444
01:10:50,620 --> 01:10:52,540
Those were the theoretical results.

1445
01:10:52,540 --> 01:10:54,863
Maybe they're really interesting.

1446
01:10:54,863 --> 01:10:57,280
But if you're actually building a machine learning system,

1447
01:10:57,280 --> 01:11:01,570
I don't know if you need to think about those things.

1448
01:11:01,570 --> 01:11:04,583
Maybe you do, which would be great.

1449
01:11:04,583 --> 01:11:06,250
But we're more just trying to expose you

1450
01:11:06,250 --> 01:11:08,433
to these different styles of thinking.

1451
01:11:08,433 --> 01:11:10,850
But now let's think a little bit about some more practical

1452
01:11:10,850 --> 01:11:12,110
considerations.

1453
01:11:12,110 --> 01:11:14,770
So the trouble is in practice, if you're actually

1454
01:11:14,770 --> 01:11:17,065
building a machine learning system,

1455
01:11:17,065 --> 01:11:18,690
we were saying that there's these three

1456
01:11:18,690 --> 01:11:20,110
pieces of the puzzle.

1457
01:11:20,110 --> 01:11:24,530
And isn't it nice if we can neatly tackle them one by one?

1458
01:11:24,530 --> 01:11:28,610
But out there in the real world, it's not like that.

1459
01:11:28,610 --> 01:11:32,970
And basically, if you're training a system,

1460
01:11:32,970 --> 01:11:34,770
you have all of these different issues

1461
01:11:34,770 --> 01:11:36,450
conflated with each other.

1462
01:11:36,450 --> 01:11:38,950
And if you've got a problem and you're training,

1463
01:11:38,950 --> 01:11:42,050
you don't know if it's because your neural network actually

1464
01:11:42,050 --> 01:11:44,670
can't approximate the thing that you're trying to approximate.

1465
01:11:44,670 --> 01:11:48,290
You don't know if it's because the training is failing.

1466
01:11:48,290 --> 01:11:50,090
Interestingly, you would know if it

1467
01:11:50,090 --> 01:11:51,667
was because it's not generalizing,

1468
01:11:51,667 --> 01:11:54,250
because you could just compute the training error and the test

1469
01:11:54,250 --> 01:11:56,083
error, and you can see if they're different.

1470
01:11:56,083 --> 01:11:59,120
So that one's easier to diagnose.

1471
01:11:59,120 --> 01:12:02,005
But the point is that all of these things are conflated.

1472
01:12:02,005 --> 01:12:03,880
And the other thing that we were going to say

1473
01:12:03,880 --> 01:12:08,000
is, suppose that you work at a large language model startup

1474
01:12:08,000 --> 01:12:10,680
and that your job is to make the LLM training as

1475
01:12:10,680 --> 01:12:13,280
efficient as possible, then you actually

1476
01:12:13,280 --> 01:12:15,160
really care about this question--

1477
01:12:15,160 --> 01:12:19,000
should I make the network wider or should I make it deeper?

1478
01:12:19,000 --> 01:12:20,520
For all those reasons that we talked

1479
01:12:20,520 --> 01:12:24,560
about-- the computational cost of training,

1480
01:12:24,560 --> 01:12:27,160
the computational cost of inference, in terms

1481
01:12:27,160 --> 01:12:30,480
of making the model work well.

1482
01:12:30,480 --> 01:12:32,480
In a sense, it's the most basic question

1483
01:12:32,480 --> 01:12:33,560
that you're thinking about when you first

1484
01:12:33,560 --> 01:12:34,727
learn about neural networks.

1485
01:12:34,727 --> 01:12:37,780
But it's, to a large extent, an unsolved problem.

1486
01:12:41,200 --> 01:12:44,600
And I wanted to talk a little bit about this paper

1487
01:12:44,600 --> 01:12:53,560
by Kaplan and McCandlish from 2020, which, this figure, people

1488
01:12:53,560 --> 01:12:55,780
got T-shirts where they just had this figure,

1489
01:12:55,780 --> 01:12:58,730
and then they would try to spread the word about.

1490
01:12:58,730 --> 01:13:01,270
And this is the figure, which is basically

1491
01:13:01,270 --> 01:13:05,650
saying as you make GPT bigger, it gets better.

1492
01:13:05,650 --> 01:13:10,910
And the point is that, in particular, the x-axis

1493
01:13:10,910 --> 01:13:13,698
is compute, the amount of flops of compute,

1494
01:13:13,698 --> 01:13:15,990
the size of your data set, and the number of parameters

1495
01:13:15,990 --> 01:13:17,070
in the model.

1496
01:13:17,070 --> 01:13:19,830
And the point is that the test loss, the y-axis,

1497
01:13:19,830 --> 01:13:24,070
is always going down as you scale any of these axes.

1498
01:13:24,070 --> 01:13:28,230
And the loss cannot go down forever because you can't have

1499
01:13:28,230 --> 01:13:29,350
negative loss.

1500
01:13:29,350 --> 01:13:30,690
The loss has to go to 0.

1501
01:13:30,690 --> 01:13:32,370
So it has to saturate eventually.

1502
01:13:32,370 --> 01:13:34,970
But back in 2020, they were saying, hey,

1503
01:13:34,970 --> 01:13:37,817
the models keep getting better.

1504
01:13:37,817 --> 01:13:39,150
We should pay attention to this.

1505
01:13:39,150 --> 01:13:43,990
Because then all of these advances in LLMs came.

1506
01:13:43,990 --> 01:13:47,430
ChatGPT was created and so on.

1507
01:13:47,430 --> 01:13:51,550
But interestingly, they plot performance against parameters.

1508
01:13:51,550 --> 01:13:53,910
In this top plot, it's not against width

1509
01:13:53,910 --> 01:13:55,590
and it's not against depth.

1510
01:13:55,590 --> 01:13:59,100
And the claim that they have in that paper

1511
01:13:59,100 --> 01:14:03,220
is that within several orders of magnitude,

1512
01:14:03,220 --> 01:14:06,820
the allocation of your compute budget between width and depth

1513
01:14:06,820 --> 01:14:07,940
doesn't matter.

1514
01:14:07,940 --> 01:14:10,500
All that matters is the number of parameters and number

1515
01:14:10,500 --> 01:14:11,260
of flops.

1516
01:14:11,260 --> 01:14:15,100
You can have a depth 20 network or a depth 10 network

1517
01:14:15,100 --> 01:14:19,400
with a commensurate number of width, it doesn't matter.

1518
01:14:19,400 --> 01:14:22,980
So that's kind of interesting, that you just run the experiment

1519
01:14:22,980 --> 01:14:26,140
and you see that width versus depth, within a large range,

1520
01:14:26,140 --> 01:14:27,320
seems not to matter.

1521
01:14:27,320 --> 01:14:31,900
And they exemplify that with this lower plot.

1522
01:14:31,900 --> 01:14:34,620
And the point is that once they subtract the embedding

1523
01:14:34,620 --> 01:14:37,680
parameters-- so if we just focus in on the bottom-right plot,

1524
01:14:37,680 --> 01:14:42,420
which is the model with the embedding layers not counted--

1525
01:14:42,420 --> 01:14:45,080
roughly you get very similar performance.

1526
01:14:45,080 --> 01:14:48,400
I think what basically they're saying-- beyond depth 6,

1527
01:14:48,400 --> 01:14:51,080
it doesn't matter what the width is or what the depth is.

1528
01:14:51,080 --> 01:14:53,380
The curves converge to each other.

1529
01:14:53,380 --> 01:14:56,770
And all that matters is scaling up number of parameters.

1530
01:14:56,770 --> 01:15:01,550
So this is just a very, very practical perspective,

1531
01:15:01,550 --> 01:15:08,210
but from some very careful experimentalists

1532
01:15:08,210 --> 01:15:10,610
about how things actually work in transformers.

1533
01:15:10,610 --> 01:15:13,370
And you see it's basically saying--

1534
01:15:13,370 --> 01:15:16,390
within a large range, width versus depth doesn't matter;

1535
01:15:16,390 --> 01:15:18,010
all you care about is parameters.

1536
01:15:18,010 --> 01:15:23,170
With that said, there's something

1537
01:15:23,170 --> 01:15:25,250
I wanted to draw attention to, which

1538
01:15:25,250 --> 01:15:28,010
is this idea of confounders.

1539
01:15:28,010 --> 01:15:29,930
And there's another paper which people

1540
01:15:29,930 --> 01:15:32,050
are also excited about, which people refer

1541
01:15:32,050 --> 01:15:34,710
to as chinchilla scaling rules.

1542
01:15:34,710 --> 01:15:37,090
The names keep getting stranger--

1543
01:15:37,090 --> 01:15:37,958
[LAUGHTER]

1544
01:15:37,958 --> 01:15:38,750
--for these things.

1545
01:15:38,750 --> 01:15:43,970
But basically, they're interested in the same sort

1546
01:15:43,970 --> 01:15:47,250
of questions as the previous paper I just showed you.

1547
01:15:47,250 --> 01:15:49,730
But what they do is they question

1548
01:15:49,730 --> 01:15:52,200
some of the results in that earlier paper.

1549
01:15:52,200 --> 01:15:54,200
And one of the things that they suggest

1550
01:15:54,200 --> 01:15:56,680
is that if you use a different learning rate

1551
01:15:56,680 --> 01:15:59,160
schedule for the training, you can

1552
01:15:59,160 --> 01:16:04,120
get slightly different, qualitatively

1553
01:16:04,120 --> 01:16:05,980
different conclusions.

1554
01:16:05,980 --> 01:16:08,160
So if you just had one learning rate schedule

1555
01:16:08,160 --> 01:16:11,060
and now you do a different learning rate schedule,

1556
01:16:11,060 --> 01:16:14,640
you draw different conclusions.

1557
01:16:14,640 --> 01:16:17,440
And this is just to say that precisely answering

1558
01:16:17,440 --> 01:16:21,800
any of these questions is very difficult,

1559
01:16:21,800 --> 01:16:24,880
because there are so many parts of the training pipeline

1560
01:16:24,880 --> 01:16:26,400
that we don't understand.

1561
01:16:26,400 --> 01:16:28,280
So you may have one experimental setup

1562
01:16:28,280 --> 01:16:31,720
and you say, hey, width versus depth doesn't matter.

1563
01:16:31,720 --> 01:16:34,080
And then you change some detail about how you actually

1564
01:16:34,080 --> 01:16:34,940
train the network.

1565
01:16:34,940 --> 01:16:37,520
And now it's like oh, no, scaling width

1566
01:16:37,520 --> 01:16:40,420
is a lot better because I fixed that issue.

1567
01:16:40,420 --> 01:16:43,360
And I would call that a confounding issue, which is just

1568
01:16:43,360 --> 01:16:45,440
some aspect of the training that we don't fully

1569
01:16:45,440 --> 01:16:49,240
understand, but is having a bearing on the results.

1570
01:16:49,240 --> 01:16:52,290
So this is just what I want to point out,

1571
01:16:52,290 --> 01:16:55,190
is that really resolving these questions

1572
01:16:55,190 --> 01:16:58,910
experimentally is really hard, because there can be

1573
01:16:58,910 --> 01:17:00,610
so many confounding variables.

1574
01:17:00,610 --> 01:17:03,450
And resolving them theoretically is also really hard,

1575
01:17:03,450 --> 01:17:06,750
because you have to work out how to think about these things.

1576
01:17:06,750 --> 01:17:09,497
And a lot of these problems are unsolved because of the fact

1577
01:17:09,497 --> 01:17:11,330
that it's kind of hard to figure things out.

1578
01:17:14,190 --> 01:17:19,110
So in conclusion, the summary.

1579
01:17:19,110 --> 01:17:21,930
Oh, yeah, there's still another slide after this one.

1580
01:17:21,930 --> 01:17:25,870
When you leave the lecture hall, try to do so quietly.

1581
01:17:25,870 --> 01:17:28,310
Someone suggested that.

1582
01:17:28,310 --> 01:17:31,170
OK, so the summary of the lecture is--

1583
01:17:34,070 --> 01:17:37,790
a very wide shallow neural net, so a three-layer

1584
01:17:37,790 --> 01:17:41,990
MLP that's wide enough can fit any function

1585
01:17:41,990 --> 01:17:44,510
within some class of functions, quite

1586
01:17:44,510 --> 01:17:46,010
a broad class of functions.

1587
01:17:49,360 --> 01:17:51,040
Then, in the second half of the lecture,

1588
01:17:51,040 --> 01:17:54,300
we said that deeper networks can fit certain kinds of functions

1589
01:17:54,300 --> 01:17:57,820
with many fewer neurons because of their compositionality.

1590
01:17:57,820 --> 01:18:00,360
And we called that a kind of depth separation result.

1591
01:18:00,360 --> 01:18:02,220
So that was something interesting.

1592
01:18:02,220 --> 01:18:12,820
And then the overall message that I am hoping to impart

1593
01:18:12,820 --> 01:18:17,940
is basically, it's unclear how important these results are.

1594
01:18:17,940 --> 01:18:20,620
It's unclear how they interact with optimization

1595
01:18:20,620 --> 01:18:24,020
and with generalization, but it's still really interesting

1596
01:18:24,020 --> 01:18:27,930
to think about them and to have that as part of your toolkit.

1597
01:18:27,930 --> 01:18:30,180
If you're thinking about your machine learning system,

1598
01:18:30,180 --> 01:18:32,460
a basic question is, can the network

1599
01:18:32,460 --> 01:18:34,020
architecture that I have actually

1600
01:18:34,020 --> 01:18:37,460
approximate the function that I'm asking it to approximate?

1601
01:18:37,460 --> 01:18:41,260
That's quite a basic question.

1602
01:18:41,260 --> 01:18:43,540
And I want to just conclude or to wrap up

1603
01:18:43,540 --> 01:18:46,220
with a little preview for what's to come in the course.

1604
01:18:46,220 --> 01:18:48,530
So I have a question.

1605
01:18:48,530 --> 01:18:51,690
And it's, suppose that we have two machine learning

1606
01:18:51,690 --> 01:18:55,290
problems-- one is an audio problem

1607
01:18:55,290 --> 01:18:58,630
and one is an image classification problem.

1608
01:18:58,630 --> 01:19:01,850
So the first little picture is like a waveform.

1609
01:19:01,850 --> 01:19:05,090
And then we're transcribing it to say the word "hello."

1610
01:19:05,090 --> 01:19:08,050
And then the second picture is like a photograph of a person.

1611
01:19:08,050 --> 01:19:12,810
And then we're classifying that as, that's a human.

1612
01:19:12,810 --> 01:19:15,550
And then the thought experiment is, in both cases,

1613
01:19:15,550 --> 01:19:21,030
we could flatten those little pieces of data into vectors.

1614
01:19:21,030 --> 01:19:23,250
OK, maybe the waveform is a vector.

1615
01:19:23,250 --> 01:19:25,570
And then we can flatten that image of a person

1616
01:19:25,570 --> 01:19:27,170
into another vector.

1617
01:19:27,170 --> 01:19:31,650
And then we can just apply MLP to both.

1618
01:19:31,650 --> 01:19:33,650
We can take a multilayer perceptron

1619
01:19:33,650 --> 01:19:37,510
with really nonlinearity and try to fit the first problem.

1620
01:19:37,510 --> 01:19:39,770
And then we can take another MLP and try

1621
01:19:39,770 --> 01:19:41,690
to fit the second problem.

1622
01:19:41,690 --> 01:19:45,640
And the argument is, OK, the MLP is a universal function

1623
01:19:45,640 --> 01:19:49,520
approximator, so this is a great idea.

1624
01:19:49,520 --> 01:19:53,786
And so I want to ask, do people think that is a good idea?

1625
01:19:53,786 --> 01:19:54,640
AUDIENCE: No!

1626
01:19:54,640 --> 01:19:55,515
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: No?

1627
01:19:55,515 --> 01:19:58,040
[LAUGHS] Why not?

1628
01:19:58,040 --> 01:20:00,760
But why not?

1629
01:20:00,760 --> 01:20:05,040
AUDIENCE: You're not guaranteed to find the approximator.

1630
01:20:05,040 --> 01:20:07,265
It exists, but you may not find it.

1631
01:20:07,265 --> 01:20:09,140
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, that's one objection.

1632
01:20:09,140 --> 01:20:11,500
That's a great point, that it could exist,

1633
01:20:11,500 --> 01:20:12,820
but we might not find it.

1634
01:20:16,240 --> 01:20:19,640
AUDIENCE: I'd say efficiency, because although it

1635
01:20:19,640 --> 01:20:21,720
is a universal function approximation

1636
01:20:21,720 --> 01:20:25,320
in the [INAUDIBLE], it says you may or may not find

1637
01:20:25,320 --> 01:20:27,760
that function in that space.

1638
01:20:27,760 --> 01:20:30,260
To find it, you may need to tune certain parameters.

1639
01:20:30,260 --> 01:20:32,660
It may take a while to find what you're looking for it.

1640
01:20:32,660 --> 01:20:37,423
So after a certain cutoff, it may not be feasible.

1641
01:20:37,423 --> 01:20:38,840
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: The objection is

1642
01:20:38,840 --> 01:20:41,100
it could be inefficient to use an MLP.

1643
01:20:41,100 --> 01:20:44,310
There might be potentially another the architecture

1644
01:20:44,310 --> 01:20:47,310
that could be more efficient.

1645
01:20:47,310 --> 01:20:50,090
And in particular, it could be adapted to the type of data.

1646
01:20:50,090 --> 01:20:51,053
Sorry?

1647
01:20:51,053 --> 01:20:52,470
AUDIENCE: I was just going to say,

1648
01:20:52,470 --> 01:20:56,990
an MLP doesn't take advantage of the structure in the input data.

1649
01:20:56,990 --> 01:20:59,430
So with an image, there's an inherent two-dimensional

1650
01:20:59,430 --> 01:21:00,370
structure.

1651
01:21:00,370 --> 01:21:03,862
And with a waveform, there's an inherent sequential structure.

1652
01:21:03,862 --> 01:21:05,570
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That's a great comment.

1653
01:21:05,570 --> 01:21:09,390
So the comment is that the two different problems

1654
01:21:09,390 --> 01:21:11,090
have different structure in the data.

1655
01:21:11,090 --> 01:21:13,598
And perhaps we want to adapt the model family.

1656
01:21:13,598 --> 01:21:15,890
And that's what I thought when I was writing the slide.

1657
01:21:15,890 --> 01:21:17,530
And then I was like, but wait a minute,

1658
01:21:17,530 --> 01:21:20,170
now we're just applying transformers to everything.

1659
01:21:20,170 --> 01:21:22,130
So is that counter to that point?

1660
01:21:22,130 --> 01:21:24,810
And then it was like, no, actually it's not.

1661
01:21:24,810 --> 01:21:27,430
Because even if you apply a transformer to audio or you

1662
01:21:27,430 --> 01:21:30,310
apply it to images, actually the preprocessing layer

1663
01:21:30,310 --> 01:21:32,830
at the beginning of the network is very different.

1664
01:21:32,830 --> 01:21:36,350
So if it's images, you have a special 2D patch kind

1665
01:21:36,350 --> 01:21:37,570
of representation.

1666
01:21:37,570 --> 01:21:39,790
And if it's audio, I assume they have some kind

1667
01:21:39,790 --> 01:21:41,790
of better audio representation.

1668
01:21:41,790 --> 01:21:44,740
So even if you just throw transformers at everything,

1669
01:21:44,740 --> 01:21:47,060
actually this comment that you do

1670
01:21:47,060 --> 01:21:50,460
adapt the architecture a little bit based on what the data is.

1671
01:21:50,460 --> 01:21:55,260
So yeah, I really agree with that comment.

1672
01:21:55,260 --> 01:21:56,820
But it was a big question.

1673
01:21:56,820 --> 01:21:59,240
People even recently have been thinking about that-- wait,

1674
01:21:59,240 --> 01:22:00,520
are transformers all we need?

1675
01:22:00,520 --> 01:22:02,760
Maybe we don't need to model the data.

1676
01:22:02,760 --> 01:22:04,500
But I think really, you still need

1677
01:22:04,500 --> 01:22:07,540
to model the data little bit.

1678
01:22:07,540 --> 01:22:11,660
And also transformers, you can ask how data hungry are they.

1679
01:22:11,660 --> 01:22:13,320
Maybe there's a better model family

1680
01:22:13,320 --> 01:22:15,750
which needs much less data in order to learn.

1681
01:22:15,750 --> 01:22:17,500
So that was the final thought to leave you

1682
01:22:17,500 --> 01:22:19,260
with is, perhaps we want to match

1683
01:22:19,260 --> 01:22:22,560
the architecture to the problem that we're trying to solve,

1684
01:22:22,560 --> 01:22:24,080
or the structure of the data.

1685
01:22:24,080 --> 01:22:26,460
Perhaps it could be more computationally efficient.

1686
01:22:26,460 --> 01:22:29,860
Perhaps it could make it easier to find the approximator

1687
01:22:29,860 --> 01:22:32,100
that we're looking for.

1688
01:22:32,100 --> 01:22:33,560
OK, that's the end of the lecture.

1689
01:22:33,560 --> 01:22:35,630
Thank you, everyone.
